    "verify": "1) List every value fed into the manifest or hash. 2) Check whether any are secrets: API keys, tokens, passwords, private keys, `.env` contents. 3) Answer no if a secret value is written in plain text to the stored manifest, or the whole environment is dumped into it."
   }
  ]
 },
 "A04": {
  "gate": "G1",
  "title": "Trustworthy attribution",
  "lead_role": "infrastructure",
  "requirement": "Assign instance and tenant identity in the trusted execution layer. Don't accept labels the model supplies. Record the delegated subject \u2014 the user or system whose authority is being used \u2014 separately from the agent's identity. Test that the agent can't pose as another instance or tenant in requests or audit records. Test that trace context survives handoffs between async jobs. Treat trace IDs and caller-supplied labels only as a way to link records. They never prove identity or permission.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Agent sharing the launching user's identity",
    "owner": "A01"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded",
    "owner": "A06"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Sub-agent authorized using an earlier actor's authority",
    "owner": "E08"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Action records missing any of the six identity fields",
    "owner": "A02"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "A04.1",
    "check": "Tenant and instance identity are set by the harness or platform from verified context, never from model-produced text or tool arguments.",
    "verify": "1) Find every place tenant and instance IDs are set for tool calls and records. 2) Check each comes from verified context (token claims, session bound at launch). 3) Check tool JSON schemas the model fills for `tenant_id`, `user_id`, `instance_id`. 4) Answer no if any function uses the model's value, even if another function uses verified context."
   },
   {
    "id": "A04.2",
    "check": "Tenant or instance labels in tool arguments or agent-controlled headers are rejected or overwritten with the verified values before they are used or logged.",
    "verify": "1) Find tool handlers and middleware that receive tool arguments and headers. 2) Check that tenant or instance fields in arguments, or headers like `X-Tenant-Id`, are rejected or overwritten with the verified value before use or logging. 3) Answer no if any handler uses or logs the supplied value, or detects a mismatch and continues."
   },
   {
    "id": "A04.3",
    "check": "The delegated subject is recorded separately from the agent identity.",
    "verify": "1) Open the audit record schema and writer. 2) Look for a delegated-subject field (the user or system whose authority is used) separate from the agent principal field. 3) Answer no if only one identity is stored, the user overwrites the agent identity, or the field is never filled."
   },
   {
    "id": "A04.4",
    "check": "When work is handed to an async job or queue, the trusted layer attaches the verified attribution and trace context, and the job uses them.",
    "verify": "1) Find queue producers and enqueue calls (Celery, SQS, Pub/Sub, Kafka, cron). 2) Check the trusted layer adds verified identity and trace context to the job. 3) Check the worker uses those values, not ones the agent wrote. 4) Answer no if workers start new traces without parent context or take tenant from agent-written job data."
   },
   {
    "id": "A04.5",
    "check": "Authorization never treats trace IDs or caller-supplied labels as proof of identity or permission.",
    "verify": "1) Find authorization and permission-lookup code. 2) Check what each decision is keyed on. 3) Answer no if any check uses a trace ID, span ID, correlation ID, request label, or caller-supplied header as identity or to look up permissions."
   }
  ]
 },
 "A05": {
  "gate": "G1",
  "title": "Operational lookup",
  "lead_role": "operations",
  "requirement": "Start from one suspicious action. Find the team responsible, the current operator, the deployment manifest, and the live instance. Show that you can contain the right instance or agent type without cutting off the launching user's other access.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Stop doesn't halt running work or open tool streams within a set time",
    "owner": "R11"
   },
   {
    "problem": "No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type",
    "owner": "E12"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Action records missing any of the six identity fields",
    "owner": "A02"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "A05.1",
    "check": "Each audit record carries the agent-type-id and agent-instance-id, which resolve to a stored deployment manifest and a live instance.",
    "verify": "1) Open the audit record writer. 2) Check `agent.type.id` and `agent.instance.id` are written on every record. 3) Check the type ID can be looked up to a stored manifest (registry, manifest store, file keyed by hash). 4) Answer no if either field is missing or nothing stores manifests by that ID. Answer unknown if the lookup store is outside this repo."
   },
   {
    "id": "A05.2",
    "check": "An ownership record in code or config maps each agent type to a responsible team and a current operator or on-call rotation.",
    "verify": "1) Look for CODEOWNERS, `catalog-info.yaml`, `.brace/deployment.yaml`, or another ownership file. 2) Check each agent type maps to a team and to a current operator or on-call rotation. 3) Answer no if an agent type has no owner, or owners exist only in prose with no link. Answer unknown if ownership lives in an outside catalog."
   },
   {
    "id": "A05.3",
    "check": "A containment control can disable a single instance or a single agent type (for example revoke its principal or scale it to zero) without touching the launching user's credentials.",
    "verify": "1) Find stop and revoke controls: admin endpoints, CLIs, feature flags, scale-to-zero, per-instance token revocation. 2) Check their scope is one instance or one agent type. 3) Answer no if the only control is a global switch or revoking a shared or user credential. Answer unknown if containment is run by an outside platform."
   }
  ]
 },
 "A06": {
  "gate": "G3",
  "title": "Parent and prompt provenance",
  "lead_role": "agent_team",
  "requirement": "For every sub-agent, record its own type and instance IDs, a link to its parent, trace links, and the prompt the parent gave it. Trace a nested action back to that prompt. Protect sensitive prompt text with restricted storage and a clear redaction policy. Standard GenAI instrumentation records prompts only when you turn that on, so confirm it is on for this path.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Sub-agent given powers beyond its task",
    "owner": "E08"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Children still running after the parent is stopped",
    "owner": "E09"
   },
   {
    "problem": "No limit on how many sub-agents a run can start",
    "owner": "B07"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "The agent never starts sub-agents (no child agents, handoffs, or delegated agent tasks).",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "A06.1",
    "check": "Each sub-agent gets its own type ID and instance ID, assigned by the harness when it is started.",
    "verify": "1) Find sub-agent spawn code (handoffs, subgraphs, subagents, A2A task creation). 2) Check the harness assigns the child a new `agent.instance.id` and a type ID at start. 3) Answer no if any spawn path reuses the parent's IDs, assigns none, or takes the IDs from the model's request."
   },
   {
    "id": "A06.2",
    "check": "Each sub-agent's records include its direct parent's instance ID and a trace link to the parent.",
    "verify": "1) Open sub-agent spawn code and the child's record writer. 2) Look for a parent instance field (for example `agent.parent.instance_id`) and trace propagation (span parent, span link, `traceparent` in queued payloads). 3) Answer no if any spawn path, including remote or queued, starts a fresh trace or omits the parent ID."
   },
   {
    "id": "A06.3",
    "check": "The prompt the parent passed to the child is captured on this path, with prompt capture switched on in config rather than left at the default off.",
    "verify": "1) Find where the parent's prompt to the child is recorded (for example `agent.parent.prompt`, or a hash plus stored text). 2) Check prompt content capture is turned on in config, for example a GenAI capture-content env var or SDK option. 3) Answer no if capture is left at its default off, or the setting falls back to false when unset."
   },
   {
    "id": "A06.4",
    "check": "Captured prompt text is stored with restricted access and a redaction policy, and only an approved audit role can read it.",
    "verify": "1) Find where captured prompt text is stored (bucket, table, log index). 2) Check its IAM or ACL limits reads to an approved audit role. 3) Check a redaction policy is defined and applied. 4) Answer no if prompts go to general logs the whole team can read, or no redaction exists. Answer unknown if storage access is set outside this repo."
   }
  ]
 },
 "A07": {
  "gate": "G3",
  "title": "Every model in the loop",
  "lead_role": "agent_team",
  "requirement": "List all helper models, such as verifiers, judges, rerankers, and world models. Give each one an identity based on its available model version or digest, its prompt, and its configuration, and record which decisions it made. Show that a swapped, drifting, or failing checker counts as a failure of the control that depends on it.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Main agent model changing behind a fixed name unnoticed",
    "owner": "C05"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Second-model review not tested against evasive behavior",
    "owner": "R10"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Self-hosted model weights loaded without a signature or format check",
    "owner": "C05"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "The agent uses no helper models at all (no verifiers, judges, rerankers, embedding models, or world models), and the model inventory shows that.",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "A07.1",
    "check": "A model inventory in code or config lists every helper model the agent calls (verifiers, judges, rerankers, embedding models, world models).",
    "verify": "1) Search for every model call: provider SDK clients, model name strings, embedding, rerank, moderation, and judge calls. 2) Compare that list with the model inventory. 3) Answer no if any helper model called in code is missing from the inventory, or no inventory exists."
   },
   {
    "id": "A07.2",
    "check": "Each helper model is identified by a pinned version or digest plus its prompt and configuration, not by an alias.",
    "verify": "1) For each helper model, find its model ID and follow env vars to the final value. 2) Check it is a pinned version or digest, and that its prompt and configuration are also recorded. 3) Answer no if any is an alias (`-latest`, undated name), falls back to one by default, or is hard-coded inline outside the inventory."
   },
   {
    "id": "A07.3",
    "check": "Each helper model's decisions are logged with that model's identity.",
    "verify": "1) Open each helper or checker call site. 2) Check its decision is logged with that model's ID and version. 3) Answer no if decisions are logged without model identity, or logged only on the allow or success branch."
   },
   {
    "id": "A07.4",
    "check": "A checker timeout, error, invalid output, or version mismatch makes the control that depends on it fail (block or escalate), not pass by default.",
    "verify": "1) Open each checker call site (judge, guardrail, moderation, verifier). 2) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, timeout, and parse-error branch. 3) Check each one blocks or escalates. 4) Answer no if any branch returns allow, skips the check, treats unparseable output as safe, or logs and continues. Also no if the returned model version is never compared with the expected one."
   }
  ]
 },
 "B01": {
  "gate": "G1",
  "title": "Minimal tool surface",
  "lead_role": "agent_team",
  "requirement": "List every built-in tool, MCP server, API, program, and file permission this agent type can use. Give a task-based reason for each. Enforce a role-based allowlist, and check that an unlisted tool can't be called. Don't base allow or approval decisions on hints a server gives about its own tools, such as \"read-only.\" MCP treats those hints as untrusted unless you trust the server.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "A tool call that skips the MCP gateway or reaches a tool server directly",
    "owner": "E06"
   },
   {
    "problem": "A token or credential with too much access",
    "owner": "B02"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Hidden instructions inside a tool's description or schema",
    "owner": "E04"
   },
   {
    "problem": "A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved",
    "owner": "E03"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Lookalike or duplicate tool names across servers",
    "owner": "E05"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "B01.1",
    "check": "Each agent role has one explicit allowlist of tool names, with no wildcard grants such as `*`, `mcp__*`, `Bash(*)`, or `bypassPermissions`.",
    "verify": "1) Find where each agent is built and tools are passed (`allowed_tools`, `tools=[`, `allowedTools`, `.mcp.json`, `settings.json`). 2) Check each role has one fixed list of names. 3) Answer no if any list holds `*`, `mcp__*`, `Bash(*)`, a pattern or regex, `bypassPermissions`, or is built from `list_tools()`, or if a second agent role or entry point has no list."
   },
   {
    "id": "B01.2",
    "check": "Tools discovered from MCP servers are filtered against that allowlist before the model can see or call them.",
    "verify": "1) Find MCP discovery (`list_tools`, `session.list_tools`, `tools/list`, MCP client setup). 2) Trace the result to where it is handed to the model. 3) Confirm a filter keeps only names on the allowlist. 4) Answer no if any discovered tool reaches the model unfiltered, the filter is a denylist or prefix match (`startswith`, `mcp__server__`), or a refresh or reconnect path skips it."
   },
   {
    "id": "B01.3",
    "check": "The dispatcher rejects a call to any tool name not on the allowlist, instead of looking it up dynamically.",
    "verify": "1) Find the dispatcher (search `tool_use`, `call_tool`, `dispatch`, `execute`, `getattr`, `registry[`). 2) Check it rejects names not on the allowlist with an error. 3) Answer no if it looks tools up with `getattr`, `globals()`, `eval`, or a registry holding more than the allowlist, falls back to calling the MCP server directly, or catches the unknown-tool error and continues."
   },
   {
    "id": "B01.4",
    "check": "Allow and approval decisions never read server-supplied hints or descriptions such as `readOnlyHint` or 'read-only'.",
    "verify": "1) Search for `readOnlyHint`, `destructiveHint`, `annotations`, `description`, and approval or auto-approve code. 2) Check whether any allow, skip-approval, or permission branch reads these. 3) Answer no if any allow or approval decision depends on a server hint or tool description text, including a default that auto-approves tools marked read-only."
   },
   {
    "id": "B01.5",
    "check": "File access is limited to named directories, not the whole filesystem.",
    "verify": "1) Find file tools and mounts (`allowed_directories`, `cwd`, `open(`, `fs.`, `os.ReadFile`, container `volumes`, `mountPath`). 2) Check paths are resolved (`realpath`, `resolve`, `filepath.Clean`) then compared to named directories. 3) Answer no if access is unrestricted, `/` or home is mounted, the check is a string `startsWith`/prefix test without resolving `..` and symlinks, or a shell tool bypasses it."
   }
  ]
 },
 "B02": {
  "gate": "G1",
  "title": "Least-privilege access",
  "lead_role": "infrastructure",
  "requirement": "Limit credentials to named operations and resources, including tenant boundaries. Test that an allowed read works. Test that an out-of-scope write, an admin action, and a request for another tenant's data all fail. Limit each token to its intended audience, so other services reject it. Remove wildcard permissions and permissions inherited from people.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "A retrieval search or cache not filtered by the requesting tenant or user",
    "owner": "R04"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Long-lived or static secrets that never expire or rotate",
    "owner": "B03"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Revocation delay from cached or self-contained tokens",
    "owner": "B04"
   },
   {
    "problem": "A sub-agent getting powers beyond its task, or a parent escalating through a peer",
    "owner": "E08"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Memory readable across tenants or tasks",
    "owner": "R05"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "B02.1",
    "check": "The agent's IAM policy or role names specific actions and resources, with no `*` action or resource and no owner, editor, or cluster-admin role.",
    "verify": "1) Find IAM and RBAC files (`*.tf`, `iam*.json`, `Role`, `ClusterRoleBinding`, `RoleBinding`, CloudFormation). 2) Find the policy attached to the agent's identity. 3) Answer no if any `Action` or `Resource` is `*` or a service wildcard (`s3:*`), or it binds `roles/owner`, `roles/editor`, `cluster-admin`, or AdministratorAccess. Answer unknown if the agent's IAM is not in this repo."
   },
   {
    "id": "B02.2",
    "check": "Tenant scope is enforced in the credential or the data layer (token claim, row-level security, or per-tenant account), not by the prompt or by code the model's output controls.",
    "verify": "1) Find how queries and API calls pick a tenant (search `tenant`, `org_id`, row-level security, `CREATE POLICY`, per-tenant credentials). 2) Trace where the tenant value comes from. 3) Answer no if one shared login or key reaches all tenants and the tenant ID comes from the prompt, a tool argument the model fills, or app code filtering after the query."
   },
   {
    "id": "B02.3",
    "check": "Tokens are issued with a set audience, and receiving services reject tokens whose audience does not match.",
    "verify": "1) Find token requests (`audience`, `aud`, `resource`, `scope`, token client setup). 2) Find token validation in receiving services (`verify`, `jwt.decode`, middleware). 3) Answer no if tokens are requested without an audience, validation skips `aud` (`verify_aud=False`, no audience option), or accepts a list or wildcard. Answer unknown if receiving services are outside this repo."
   },
   {
    "id": "B02.4",
    "check": "The agent uses its own service identity, not a human user's token, a copied session, or an admin key.",
    "verify": "1) Find where the agent loads credentials (env vars, `credentials`, `token`, `Authorization`, service account settings). 2) Check they belong to a service or workload identity for the agent. 3) Answer no if it uses a user's OAuth or session token, a personal access token, an admin or root key, or forwards the calling user's token to tools."
   }
  ]
 },
 "B03": {
  "gate": "G2",
  "title": "Credential lifetime and handling",
  "lead_role": "infrastructure",
  "requirement": "Issue short-lived credentials from a managed identity or secrets service. Keep long-lived secrets out of prompts, images, and checked-in config. Where the service supports it, bind tokens to their holder (mTLS, DPoP, or workload proof tokens). Then a copied token won't work from another machine. Don't use fixed API keys when short-lived or federated credentials are available. Show that credentials expire and rotate, and explain why you chose their lifetime.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "A credential scoped to more operations, resources, or tenants than needed",
    "owner": "B02"
   },
   {
    "problem": "No way to cut off the agent's access without cutting off the user",
    "owner": "B04"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Secrets showing up in tool output, responses, or normal logs",
    "owner": "R03"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Container image not pinned by digest or signature-checked",
    "owner": "B10"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "B03.1",
    "check": "Credentials are fetched at run time from a managed identity or secrets service, not fixed API keys, where short-lived or federated credentials are available.",
    "verify": "1) Find credential setup (`AssumeRole`, IRSA, Workload Identity, Managed Identity, Vault, `os.environ`, `process.env`). 2) Check each credential is fetched at run time. 3) Answer no if a fixed key (`AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `API_KEY` in a Secret or env) is used for a service that offers federated or short-lived credentials, or code falls back to a static key when the managed source fails."
   },
   {
    "id": "B03.2",
    "check": "Credential lifetime and rotation are set in config to bounded values.",
    "verify": "1) Search for lifetime and rotation settings (`DurationSeconds`, `ttl`, `expires_in`, `max_ttl`, rotation job or policy). 2) Check each credential the agent uses has a bounded value. 3) Answer no if any credential has no expiry, no rotation, or an unbounded or very long value. Answer unknown if lifetimes are set in an identity provider outside this repo."
   },
   {
    "id": "B03.3",
    "check": "No long-lived secrets appear in prompts, Dockerfiles or image layers, or checked-in config.",
    "verify": "1) Search prompt templates, Dockerfiles (`ENV`, `ARG`, `COPY .env`), `.env*`, `*.yaml`, `*.tfvars`, `.mcp.json` `env` blocks for key-like literals (`sk-`, `AKIA`, `token:`, `password:`). 2) Check prompt-building code for env values inserted into messages. 3) Answer no if any real secret is checked in, baked into an image, or placed in a prompt; `.gitignore` listing a file does not count if it is committed."
   },
   {
    "id": "B03.4",
    "check": "Where the service supports it, tokens are bound to their holder with mTLS, DPoP, or workload proof tokens.",
    "verify": "1) Find token and client setup for each service (`mTLS`, `client_cert`, `DPoP`, `cert-bound`, SPIFFE). 2) Check whether the service supports holder binding. 3) Answer no if a supporting service uses plain bearer tokens. Answer unknown if support depends on a provider outside this repo and nothing states it."
   }
  ]
 },
 "B04": {
  "gate": "G1",
  "title": "Independent revocation",
  "lead_role": "infrastructure",
  "requirement": "Show that an operator can cut off this agent's access without cutting off the user who launched it. The agent must not need to cooperate. Set and measure the longest time before the cutoff takes effect. Count cached tokens, self-contained tokens, and open sessions. A trusted enforcement point must block later calls within a time limit that fits the potential harm. Revoking only the refresh token is not enough. If tokens live longer than that limit, push the revocation to each service (for example, with OpenID CAEP session-revoked events) or make the tokens shorter-lived.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "A kill switch that doesn't stop running work or open tool streams",
    "owner": "R11"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Sub-agents or remote workers still acting after the parent is stopped",
    "owner": "E09"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Static API keys used where short-lived credentials are available",
    "owner": "B03"
   },
   {
    "problem": "No way to trace a suspicious action to its team, operator, and live instance",
    "owner": "A05"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "B04.1",
    "check": "A revocation control disables the agent's own identity or credentials without disabling the user who launched it.",
    "verify": "1) Find a disable or revoke path (script, runbook, IdP config, `disable`, `revoke`, deny list). 2) Check it targets the agent's own identity or credential. 3) Answer no if the agent uses the user's token, so revocation disables the user too, or no revoke path exists. Answer unknown if revocation lives only in an identity provider outside this repo."
   },
   {
    "id": "B04.2",
    "check": "Revocation is applied at a trusted enforcement point (identity provider, gateway, or target service) and does not need the agent's cooperation.",
    "verify": "1) Find where revocation is checked (gateway, auth middleware, IdP, introspection, deny list lookup). 2) Check it runs outside the agent process. 3) Answer no if revocation sends a message or signal to the agent and relies on it to drop credentials, or the check runs inside agent code."
   },
   {
    "id": "B04.3",
    "check": "Later calls are blocked even with cached tokens, self-contained tokens, and open sessions: token lifetime is shorter than the revocation limit, or revocation is pushed to each service (for example CAEP) or checked on every call.",
    "verify": "1) Find access-token lifetimes and how services validate tokens (signature only vs `introspect`, deny list, CAEP). 2) Compare lifetime with the stated revocation limit. 3) Answer no if self-contained JWTs outlive the limit and are checked by signature only, cached tokens or open sessions skip the check, or no limit is stated. Answer unknown if validation lives outside this repo."
   },
   {
    "id": "B04.4",
    "check": "Revocation covers access tokens and open sessions, not only the refresh token.",
    "verify": "1) Find the revoke code or runbook. 2) List what it revokes: refresh token, access tokens, sessions, streams. 3) Answer no if it only revokes the refresh token or deletes stored credentials while issued access tokens and open sessions stay valid."
   }
  ]
 },
 "B05": {
  "gate": "G1",
  "title": "Destructive-action interception",
  "lead_role": "agent_team",
  "requirement": "List the high-impact operations. These include deletes, destructive database updates, force-pushes, tearing down infrastructure, payment changes, and sending anything outside. Block them by default in the execution path. Allow one only when a reviewer or system with higher authority approves that specific action. When the action runs, authorize its operation, target, and arguments, and block it if they aren't allowed. A list of blocked words or command names is not enough. Test other tools and raw API or shell paths that could do the same thing. Treat deleting audit records, or changing how long they are kept, as high-impact too. That includes asking a more powerful tool or peer to do it. Some agents can do all three of these in one context: read untrusted input, reach sensitive data or systems, and change things or talk to the outside. For those agents, treat every consequential action as high-impact. The exception is a design that stops untrusted content from picking the tool, destination, or arguments. Examples are plan-then-execute, a two-model pattern, or capability tracking such as CaMeL. Record which of the three each agent type has.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Denied actions not recorded in the audit trail",
    "owner": "R13"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Approvals that can be replayed, reused in parallel, or used after expiry",
    "owner": "B06"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Outside effects left unreconciled after the agent is stopped",
    "owner": "R12"
   },
   {
    "problem": "A chain of individually allowed actions that adds up to data theft",
    "owner": "R09"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Tool output not labelled or kept apart from trusted instructions",
    "owner": "R02"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "B05.1",
    "check": "High-impact operations are listed in code or config and blocked by default, including deletes, destructive updates, force-pushes, infrastructure teardown, payment changes, outbound sends, and deleting audit records or changing their retention.",
    "verify": "1) Search code and config for a list of high-impact operations (deny/approval policy, OPA/Rego, Cedar, hook config). 2) Check it covers deletes, destructive updates, force-push, teardown, payments, outbound sends, audit deletion or retention changes. 3) Answer no if no list exists, any category is missing with a tool that can do it, or unlisted operations default to `allow`."
   },
   {
    "id": "B05.2",
    "check": "The gate runs in the execution path when the call runs and checks the operation, the target, and the arguments, not only a tool name, command name, or blocklist of words.",
    "verify": "1) Find where tool calls execute (`tool_use`, `call_tool`, `execute`, `can_use_tool`, `PreToolUse`). 2) Find the gate that runs before the side effect and see what it inspects. 3) Answer no if it checks only the tool or command name, matches words (`'rm' in cmd`, `DROP`), runs after the call, lives only in the prompt, or sits in a `try`/`catch` that continues on error. 4) For each value that lets an action skip approval (an internal recipient, a small amount, a safe path), read the comparison itself: answer no if a domain, host, or path is matched by suffix, prefix, or substring without an anchor, so `endswith('example.com')` also passes `evil-example.com`."
   },
   {
    "id": "B05.3",
    "check": "The same gate covers every path that can do the operation, including raw shell, HTTP or API clients, other tools, and requests to a more powerful tool or peer agent.",
    "verify": "1) Open the tool registry or config and write every tool the model can call into your note, by name, including MCP servers and sub-agents. 2) For each, say whether it can do a gated operation, including through a page it controls: a browser click or form fill in a logged-in session can buy, send, or delete. 3) Trace each such tool to the gate. 4) Answer no if any alias, raw shell or HTTP tool, browser action, second dispatch path, peer agent, or more powerful MCP tool can do the operation without passing the same gate."
   },
   {
    "id": "B05.4",
    "check": "A blocked operation runs only after approval from a person or system with higher authority than the agent; a prompt, comment, or model instruction is never the enforcement.",
    "verify": "1) Find what happens when the gate blocks (approval request, `interrupt()`, `needs_approval`, webhook to reviewer). 2) Check who approves. 3) Answer no if the model, the agent itself, or a prompt instruction can approve, if approval is auto-granted (`bypassPermissions`, `acceptEdits`, auto-approve flag, `yes` default), or if a timeout proceeds."
   },
   {
    "id": "B05.5",
    "check": "For an agent that reads untrusted input, reaches sensitive data or systems, and acts outside, every consequential action is gated, unless code shows untrusted content cannot pick the tool, destination, or arguments (for example plan-then-execute or capability tracking).",
    "verify": "1) Check whether the agent reads untrusted content (web, email, files, tool output), reaches sensitive data, and can act outside. 2) If all three, list consequential actions and check each is gated. 3) Answer no if any is ungated and untrusted content can choose its tool, destination, or arguments with no plan-then-execute or capability tracking in code. Answer yes if the three are not combined."
   }
  ]
 },
 "B06": {
  "gate": "G1",
  "title": "Approval integrity",
  "lead_role": "agent_team",
  "requirement": "Tie each approval to the agent instance that asked, and to the exact operation, target, arguments, and scope. Record who approved it and when it expires. Show the reviewer what the action will do. Make approvals single-use, or set clear limits on a broader grant. When the action runs, check permissions and the relevant resource state again. Record each use of an approval in one step, so parallel calls can't go past the grant. Test changed arguments, a different agent instance, expired approvals, replay, parallel reuse, and approvers without authority. If the approval service fails, the action must not quietly go ahead.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "A high-impact action that never asks for approval at all",
    "owner": "B05"
   },
   {
    "problem": "A parent gaining a denied power by asking a stronger peer",
    "owner": "E08"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Harness or approval code changed in production without review",
    "owner": "B12"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "Only when the agent has no actions that need approval under B05, with that inventory recorded.",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "B06.1",
    "check": "Each approval record is bound to the requesting agent instance and to the exact operation, target, arguments, and scope, and records the approver and an expiry time.",
    "verify": "1) Find the approval data model (schema, class, table, `approval`). 2) List its fields. 3) Answer no if it lacks any of agent instance ID, operation, target, exact arguments or hash, scope, approver, and expiry, or if approval is a boolean like `approved=True`."
   },
   {
    "id": "B06.2",
    "check": "When the action runs, the gate rejects it if the instance, operation, target, or arguments differ from the approval, or if the approval has expired.",
    "verify": "1) Find where an approved action executes. 2) Check the code compares instance, operation, target, and arguments (or hash) against the stored approval and checks expiry at that moment. 3) Answer no if approval is checked only when requested, any field is not compared, arguments are re-read from the model after the check, or expiry is never checked."
   },
   {
    "id": "B06.3",
    "check": "Each use of an approval is recorded in one atomic step (single-use or a bounded counter), so replays and parallel calls cannot go past the grant.",
    "verify": "1) Find where an approval is used or marked consumed. 2) Check it is one atomic step: transaction with lock, compare-and-set, unique constraint, or atomic decrement. 3) Answer no if it reads then writes separately, never marks use, or keeps the count in process memory that parallel workers do not share."
   },
   {
    "id": "B06.4",
    "check": "The gate checks that the approver has authority for this action, and rechecks permissions and the relevant resource state when the action runs.",
    "verify": "1) Find approver checks (role list, `can_approve`, authority mapping). 2) Find rechecks of permissions and resource state at execution. 3) Answer no if any logged-in user can approve, the approver is not checked against the action, or permissions and resource state are not rechecked when the action runs."
   },
   {
    "id": "B06.5",
    "check": "If the approval service errors or cannot be reached, the action is denied.",
    "verify": "1) Find calls to the approval service. 2) Read their error, timeout, and exception handling. 3) Answer no if a `try`/`except` or `catch` logs and proceeds, the default on error or timeout is allow, or a missing response is treated as approved."
   }
  ]
 },
 "B07": {
  "gate": "G2",
  "title": "Hard execution budgets",
  "lead_role": "agent_team",
  "requirement": "Enforce limits outside the model on loop count, run time, tokens, cost, tool-call rate, and how many sub-agents it can start. Also set spend and rate quotas per user or service, per tenant, and across the whole fleet. That way, many small runs can't use up shared capacity. Hit each limit in a test, and record how the run stops or escalates.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Budget or cross-agent abuse alerts not reaching the team that can act",
    "owner": "E12"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Unusual fan-out or resource use going undetected",
    "owner": "R09"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Context size not logged for each action decision",
    "owner": "R07"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "B07.1",
    "check": "Limits on loop count, run time, tokens, cost, tool-call rate, and number of sub-agents started are set in code or config.",
    "verify": "1) Search for `max_turns`, `recursion_limit`, `max_iterations`, `timeout`, `max_tokens`, cost or budget caps, rate limits, sub-agent caps. 2) Check each of the six limits has a value. 3) Answer no if any is missing, set to unlimited (`None`, `0`, `-1`, `inf`), or the loop is `while True` with no cap."
   },
   {
    "id": "B07.2",
    "check": "The harness or a gateway outside the model enforces each limit and stops or escalates the run when it is hit; the model cannot raise, reset, or skip them.",
    "verify": "1) For each limit, find the code that enforces it and what happens when hit. 2) Check it runs in the harness or gateway. 3) Answer no if a limit is only in the prompt, can be changed by a tool argument or model output, is caught and the run continues, or hitting it has no stop or escalation."
   },
   {
    "id": "B07.3",
    "check": "Budgets apply to nested sub-agents, so their usage counts against the limits.",
    "verify": "1) Find sub-agent spawning (`spawn`, `Task`, `handoff`, `create_agent`). 2) Check child runs share or deduct from the parent budget and depth is capped. 3) Answer no if each child gets a fresh budget, nested spawns are uncounted, or depth and count are unlimited."
   },
   {
    "id": "B07.4",
    "check": "Spend and rate quotas also apply per user or service, per tenant, and across the fleet, counted across runs, not only within one run.",
    "verify": "1) Search gateway, LLM proxy (LiteLLM budgets), and provider config for per-user, per-tenant, and fleet quotas. 2) Check counts persist across runs (shared store, not process memory). 3) Answer no if only per-run limits exist. Answer unknown if quotas would live in a provider console or gateway outside this repo."
   }
  ]
 },
 "B08": {
  "gate": "G2",
  "title": "Environment isolation",
  "lead_role": "infrastructure",
  "requirement": "Write down every system and data store the agent can reach. Test that it has no path to unapproved production resources, admin systems, host services, or other tenants. Enforce this in the infrastructure, not only in the prompt. For each allowed connection, also check input and response validation under R01\u2013R02, including API responses and messages from connected services. Being allowed on the network does not make their content trusted.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Outbound traffic to unapproved internet destinations",
    "owner": "B09"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Container running as root or shipping a shell it doesn't need",
    "owner": "B11"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Content from an allowed service trusted without validation",
    "owner": "R01"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Agent reaching an approved MCP server directly instead of through the gateway",
    "owner": "E06"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "B08.1",
    "check": "Network policy or security groups default-deny and allow only the listed systems and data stores the agent needs.",
    "verify": "1) Find NetworkPolicy, security groups, firewall rules, docker-compose networks. 2) Check for a default deny and named allowed peers. 3) Answer no if the agent's namespace has no policy, rules allow all, `0.0.0.0/0` or a broad CIDR is allowed, or allowed peers go beyond the listed systems. Answer unknown if network config lives outside this repo."
   },
   {
    "id": "B08.2",
    "check": "No route, mount, or credential gives the runtime a path to unapproved production resources, admin systems, host services, or other tenants. Egress to 0.0.0.0/0 or another broad range, even only on ports 80 and 443, is such a route: it reaches cloud metadata (169.254.169.254) and internal services unless they are explicitly blocked.",
    "verify": "1) List routes, mounts, and credentials in the agent's runtime (egress rules, volumes, env secrets, `hostNetwork`, `docker.sock`). 2) Check each reaches only approved systems. 3) Answer no if egress allows `0.0.0.0/0` or a broad range on any port without blocking 169.254.169.254, if host services or the Docker socket are reachable, or credentials reach production admin or other tenants."
   },
   {
    "id": "B08.3",
    "check": "Isolation is enforced in infrastructure config, not by the prompt or by agent code the model can influence.",
    "verify": "1) Find where isolation is defined. 2) Check it is in infrastructure (NetworkPolicy, VPC, IAM, separate namespace or account). 3) Answer no if it relies on prompt text or agent code (URL checks, host lists in the harness) that the model's output can steer, with no infrastructure rule behind it."
   }
  ]
 },
 "B09": {
  "gate": "G2",
  "title": "Egress allowlist",
  "lead_role": "infrastructure",
  "requirement": "Allow only the outside destinations the task needs. Test a blocked destination and ways around the block, such as going straight past a proxy or gateway. Cover redirects, DNS changes and rebinding, IPv4 and IPv6, and cloud metadata or loopback addresses where they apply. An allowed domain can still host an attacker's account or receive leaked data. So also limit which resources and tenants the agent can reach, and what data it can send. Record each block and confirm the right operator hears about it.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Data leaking through client-side Markdown images or link previews",
    "owner": "R03"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Network path to internal production or admin systems",
    "owner": "B08"
   },
   {
    "problem": "A loosened egress rule in the running setup raising no alert",
    "owner": "C04"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "B09.1",
    "check": "Outbound traffic is default-deny, with a destination allowlist enforced at a proxy, gateway, or network policy.",
    "verify": "1) Find egress rules (NetworkPolicy egress, Cilium FQDN, proxy ACLs, cloud firewall). 2) Check outbound is default deny with a destination allowlist. 3) Answer no if egress allows `0.0.0.0/0` or broad CIDRs, no egress rules exist, or the allowlist is only in app code the agent runs. Answer unknown if egress is set outside this repo."
   },
   {
    "id": "B09.2",
    "check": "Network policy blocks direct routes around the proxy or gateway, for both IPv4 and IPv6.",
    "verify": "1) Check whether the proxy is enforced by network policy or only by `HTTP_PROXY`/`HTTPS_PROXY` env. 2) Check IPv6 egress rules. 3) Answer no if pods can reach the internet except via the proxy, the proxy is only an env var, `NO_PROXY` is broad, or IPv6 is not restricted."
   },
   {
    "id": "B09.3",
    "check": "Redirect targets and resolved IP addresses are checked against the allowlist, and cloud metadata and loopback addresses are blocked.",
    "verify": "1) Find fetch and HTTP client code (`requests`, `fetch`, `http.Client`, `follow_redirects`, `allow_redirects`). 2) Check each redirect hop and resolved IP is checked. 3) Answer no if redirects are followed unchecked, the check uses the hostname but not the resolved IP, or 169.254.169.254, fd00:ec2::254, or 127.0.0.0/8 and ::1 are not blocked."
   },
   {
    "id": "B09.4",
    "check": "Allowed destinations are limited by resource, tenant, or path where needed, not only by domain.",
    "verify": "1) List allowed destinations that are shared services (S3, GitHub, storage, paste sites). 2) Check rules limit bucket, account, repo, tenant, or path. 3) Answer no if a shared service is allowed by domain or wildcard (`*.s3.amazonaws.com`) alone, or path checks use string prefix without normalising."
   },
   {
    "id": "B09.5",
    "check": "Each blocked request is logged and routed as an alert to a named operator.",
    "verify": "1) Find where blocked requests are logged (proxy deny log, policy audit, app code). 2) Find the alert route to a named operator. 3) Answer no if blocks are not logged, only allowed requests are logged, or no alert route names an operator. Answer unknown if alerting lives outside this repo."
   }
  ]
 },
 "B10": {
  "gate": "G2",
  "title": "Pinned, verified image",
  "lead_role": "infrastructure",
  "requirement": "Pin the container by digest. Before it runs, check its digest and signature against an approved signing key, or an approved certificate identity and issuer. Keep its build provenance (the record of how it was built) and its list of dependencies. Check provenance fields such as the builder, source repository, build type, and the parameters set from outside the build (SLSA `externalParameters`) against what you expect. Keep the verifier patched. Cosign versions before 2.6.2 and 3.0.4 could accept a bundle whose transparency-log entry didn't match the artifact. Confirm that the admission check covers production namespaces and is set to enforce, not just audit. Show that an unapproved or changed image is rejected.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Tool or MCP server package not vetted or pinned",
    "owner": "E02"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Model weights or adapters loaded without a signature or format check",
    "owner": "C05"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Image runs as root or includes a shell or package manager",
    "owner": "B11"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Secrets baked into the image",
    "owner": "B03"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "Only when the deployment runs no container image you build or choose (for example, a fully vendor-hosted agent). An unpinned or unsigned image is a Gap, not N/A.",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "B10.1",
    "check": "Production manifests reference images by digest (`@sha256:`), not by tag alone.",
    "verify": "1) Find every `image:` field in manifests, Helm values, compose, and Terraform. 2) Check each uses `@sha256:`. 3) Answer no if any production image uses only a tag (`:latest`, `:v1`), or a template defaults to a tag when the digest value is empty."
   },
   {
    "id": "B10.2",
    "check": "An admission policy verifies the signature against a pinned key, or a pinned certificate identity and issuer, with no wildcard identity or issuer.",
    "verify": "1) Find admission policies (Kyverno `verifyImages`, `ClusterImagePolicy`, Gatekeeper). 2) Check the key or certificate identity and issuer are pinned. 3) Answer no if identity or issuer uses `.*` or wildcard regex, issuer is unset, or no admission policy exists. Answer unknown if admission config is outside this repo."
   },
   {
    "id": "B10.3",
    "check": "The policy checks provenance fields: builder, source repository, build type, and external parameters.",
    "verify": "1) In the admission policy, find attestation or provenance conditions. 2) Check builder ID, source repository, build type, and `externalParameters` are each compared to expected values. 3) Answer no if any is missing, provenance is only generated in CI but not verified, or conditions use wildcards."
   },
   {
    "id": "B10.4",
    "check": "The policy is in enforce mode, covers production namespaces, and denies images that match no policy.",
    "verify": "1) Read the policy mode and scope (`validationFailureAction`, `failureAction`, `mode`, namespace selectors, exclusions). 2) Check for a no-match deny. 3) Answer no if mode is `Audit` or `warn`, production namespaces are excluded or unmatched, `failurePolicy: Ignore`, or images matching no policy are allowed."
   },
   {
    "id": "B10.5",
    "check": "The pinned Cosign or verifier version is at least 2.6.2 (2.x) or 3.0.4 (3.x).",
    "verify": "1) Find Cosign or verifier versions in CI workflows (`cosign-installer`, `cosign-release`), Dockerfiles, and policy-controller charts. 2) Compare to 2.6.2 (2.x) and 3.0.4 (3.x). 3) Answer no if any pin is lower or uses `latest` or no version. Answer unknown if no verifier is in this repo."
   }
  ]
 },
 "B11": {
  "gate": "G2",
  "title": "Minimal, isolated runtime",
  "lead_role": "infrastructure",
  "requirement": "Remove shells, downloaders, package managers, mounts, and privileges the agent doesn't need. As a container baseline, meet the Kubernetes Pod Security Standards \"restricted\" profile or the same controls elsewhere. That means non-root, no privilege escalation, all capabilities dropped, and a seccomp profile set. Where supported, also use a read-only root filesystem and user namespaces; the restricted profile doesn't require those two. For running untrusted code, use a sandbox that fits the threat, such as a microVM or a user-space kernel. Ordinary containers share the host's kernel. For managed runtimes, record the provider's isolation evidence and what you can't see. Test that host access is blocked. A blocked probe does not prove the sandbox resists every escape.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Container image not pinned by digest or signature-checked",
    "owner": "B10"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Outbound traffic to unapproved internet destinations",
    "owner": "B09"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Network path to admin systems or other tenants' resources",
    "owner": "B08"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "B11.1",
    "check": "Container settings set non-root, no privilege escalation, all capabilities dropped, and a seccomp profile, matching Pod Security 'restricted' or the same controls.",
    "verify": "1) Find pod `securityContext` and container `securityContext`, plus namespace labels. 2) Check `runAsNonRoot: true`, `allowPrivilegeEscalation: false`, `capabilities.drop: [ALL]`, `seccompProfile: RuntimeDefault`. 3) Answer no if any is missing on any container (including init and sidecars), set the wrong way, or overridden per container, or the Dockerfile runs as root with no override."
   },
   {
    "id": "B11.2",
    "check": "The final image has no shell, downloader, or package manager the agent does not need.",
    "verify": "1) Read the final stage of each Dockerfile (after the last `FROM`). 2) Check base image and installs for `bash`, `sh`, `curl`, `wget`, `apt`, `apk`, `pip`, `npm`. 3) Answer no if the final image has any the agent does not need, or the final stage is a full OS image (ubuntu, debian, python) not distroless or scratch."
   },
   {
    "id": "B11.3",
    "check": "There is no privileged mode, hostPath or Docker socket mount, or host network or PID namespace.",
    "verify": "1) Search manifests and compose for `privileged`, `hostPath`, `docker.sock`, `hostNetwork`, `hostPID`, `hostIPC`, `network_mode: host`, `pid: host`. 2) Answer no if any appears on the agent's workloads, including sidecars and init containers."
   },
   {
    "id": "B11.4",
    "check": "If the agent runs untrusted or model-written code, that code runs in a separate sandbox such as gVisor, Kata, or a microVM, not in the agent's own container.",
    "verify": "1) Find code execution tools (`subprocess`, `exec`, `eval`, `child_process`, `os/exec`, code interpreter). 2) Check where that code runs (`runtimeClassName: gvisor`, Kata, Firecracker, remote sandbox API). 3) Answer no if model-written or untrusted code runs in the agent's own process or container, or the sandbox call falls back to local execution on error. Answer yes if no such code runs."
   }
  ]
 },
 "B12": {
  "gate": "G1",
  "title": "Reviewed harness and instructions",
  "lead_role": "agent_team",
  "requirement": "Version and review the harness, system prompt, and rules files before release. Allow production changes only through a release process that records who made them. Protect source branches from history rewrites, so the reviewed diff stays available. Link the deployed versions to the approved review. Complete the full configuration checks in section C.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Running config drifting from the approved baseline",
    "owner": "C04"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Model or dependency change promoted without review and re-testing",
    "owner": "C05"
   },
   {
    "problem": "A tool server changing its description or schema after approval",
    "owner": "E03"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Release candidate not re-tested against attack cases",
    "owner": "C06"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "B12.1",
    "check": "The harness, system prompt, and rules files are in version control and loaded from the reviewed build, not fetched at run time from a database, URL, or feature flag.",
    "verify": "1) Find where the system prompt and rules are loaded (`system_prompt`, `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `prompts/`). 2) Check they come from files in the repo or build. 3) Answer no if any part is fetched at run time from a database, URL, feature flag, or env var with no recorded version, or a remote source overrides the file when available."
   },
   {
    "id": "B12.2",
    "check": "CODEOWNERS or required-review rules cover the prompt, rules, and harness files.",
    "verify": "1) Read `CODEOWNERS` and review rules (`.github/`, rulesets, branch protection IaC). 2) Check patterns match prompt, rules, and harness paths. 3) Answer no if those paths are uncovered, the pattern misses them (wrong glob, case), or no CODEOWNERS exists. Answer unknown if required reviews are set only in repo settings outside the code."
   },
   {
    "id": "B12.3",
    "check": "Production deploys run only from CI on the reviewed branch and record the commit, tag, or digest deployed.",
    "verify": "1) Read deploy workflows and scripts. 2) Check deploy triggers only on the reviewed branch and records commit, tag, or digest. 3) Answer no if `workflow_dispatch` or a script deploys any branch or local build, deploy scripts accept manual laptop pushes, or no version is recorded. Answer unknown if deploy is outside this repo."
   },
   {
    "id": "B12.4",
    "check": "Branch protection config blocks force-pushes and history rewrites on the source branch.",
    "verify": "1) Search for branch protection IaC (`github_branch_protection`, rulesets JSON). 2) Check force-push and deletion are blocked on the source branch. 3) Answer no if `allow_force_pushes: true` or bypass is open to the agent's identity. Answer unknown if protection is set only in repo settings and not in code."
   }
  ]
 },
 "C01": {
  "gate": "G2",
  "title": "Complete release manifest",
  "lead_role": "agent_team",
  "requirement": "Write a release manifest: a list of everything that makes up this release. Include the container, harness, system prompt, rules files, model, built-in tools, MCP servers, permission scopes, memory and retrieval settings, identity bindings, network rules, and execution budgets. Include security-policy versions. Add a separate model record for every model: the main agent model, sub-agent models, and helper models such as embedding models and rerankers. For each, record the provider, model ID, exact version or checkpoint, and any adapter or fine-tune version. For training or fine-tuning you run yourself, also record the training job ID, base-model version, training code and config version, dataset version, and the digest of the output. The agent app's release number alone is not enough. Use the [model provenance fields](CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md#model-provenance-fields) to separate recorded versions from details a provider doesn't share. Check each model ID you request against the provider's versioning docs. Mark it as a pinned snapshot or as an alias that can change, and record which doc you used and its date. Naming rules differ by provider and model generation. Name each artifact's owner and a reference that can't change, where one exists. A machine-readable format, such as CycloneDX ML-BOM or the SPDX 3.0 AI and Dataset profiles, lets tools check the manifest.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Running setup not checked against the approved manifest",
    "owner": "C03"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Manifest changes made without a reviewed diff and named author",
    "owner": "C02"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Running config drifting from the approved baseline unnoticed",
    "owner": "C04"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Agent-type-id not computed as a hash of its identity inputs",
    "owner": "A03"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "C01.1",
    "check": "A release manifest file lists the container, harness, system prompt, rules files, model, built-in tools, MCP servers, permission scopes, memory and retrieval settings, identity bindings, network rules, execution budgets, and security-policy versions.",
    "verify": "1) Find the release manifest (`.brace/manifest.yaml`, release manifest, ML-BOM). 2) Check each category: container, harness, system prompt, rules files, model, built-in tools, MCP servers, permission scopes, memory and retrieval, identity bindings, network rules, budgets, security-policy versions. 3) Compare with what config sets up (`*.mcp.json`, NetworkPolicy). 4) Answer no if any category is missing or configured items are left out.",
    "doc_ok": true
   },
   {
    "id": "C01.2",
    "check": "The manifest has a separate model record for every model (main, sub-agent, and helper models such as embedding models and rerankers), each with provider, model ID, exact version or checkpoint, and adapter or fine-tune version.",
    "verify": "1) Search code for every model used: main, sub-agent, embedding, reranker, judge. 2) Check the manifest has a separate record for each. 3) Check each record has provider, model ID, exact version or checkpoint, and adapter or fine-tune version. 4) Answer no if any model in code has no record, or a record is missing a field.",
    "doc_ok": true
   },
   {
    "id": "C01.3",
    "check": "Each requested model ID is labeled as a pinned snapshot or a changeable alias, with the provider doc and date used.",
    "verify": "1) Open each model record in the manifest. 2) Check it has a pinned-or-alias label, the provider doc used, and a date. 3) Answer no if any record lacks one of these, or labels an undated or `-latest` name as pinned."
   },
   {
    "id": "C01.4",
    "check": "Each artifact has a named owner and a reference that can't change (digest or commit) where one exists, not a mutable tag or the app's release number.",
    "verify": "1) For each manifest artifact, check for a named owner. 2) Check its reference is a digest (`sha256:`) or commit SHA where one exists. 3) Answer no if any artifact has no owner, or uses a mutable tag (`:latest`, branch name) or the app release number where a digest or commit was available."
   },
   {
    "id": "C01.5",
    "check": "For training the team runs itself, the manifest records the training job ID, base-model version, training code and config version, dataset version, and output digest.",
    "verify": "1) Search for training or fine-tuning code (training jobs, `Trainer`, fine-tune API calls, pipeline configs). 2) If found, check the manifest records job ID, base-model version, training code and config version, dataset version, and output digest. 3) Answer no if training runs here and any field is missing. Answer unknown if training may run in another repo."
   }
  ]
 },
 "C02": {
  "gate": "G2",
  "title": "Review security-relevant changes",
  "lead_role": "governance",
  "requirement": "Require diffs with a named author, plus review, for changes to the manifest and its policies. Show who is allowed to change production config. Record emergency changes with an owner, a reason, an end date, and a follow-up review.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Running config drifting from the approved baseline unnoticed",
    "owner": "C04"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Release candidate not re-tested against attack cases",
    "owner": "C06"
   },
   {
    "problem": "No tested rollback to an approved setup",
    "owner": "C07"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "C02.1",
    "check": "The manifest, prompts, tool config, and policy files live in version control, and merges require review by someone other than the named author.",
    "verify": "1) Check the manifest, prompts, tool config, and policy files are tracked in git, not loaded at runtime from an untracked path or remote URL. 2) Look for branch protection or ruleset config requiring approving review, and CODEOWNERS covering those paths. 3) Answer no if any of these files is untracked or not covered. Answer unknown if review rules are set only in the hosting service."
   },
   {
    "id": "C02.2",
    "check": "Only named roles in config (CODEOWNERS, IAM, or protected CI environments) can change production configuration.",
    "verify": "1) Open CODEOWNERS, IAM policies, and CI environment config (protected `environment:` entries). 2) Check production config paths and deploy jobs are limited to named roles. 3) Answer no if a catch-all `*` owner, a broad group, or the agent's own credentials can change production config, or a deploy job skips the protected environment."
   },
   {
    "id": "C02.3",
    "check": "An emergency change path exists that records an owner, a reason, an end date, and a follow-up review.",
    "verify": "1) Search for an emergency or break-glass path (runbook, workflow, CLI). 2) Check it records owner, reason, end date, and follow-up review. 3) Answer no if any of the four is missing, or the path bypasses review with no record. Answer unknown if the procedure lives outside this repo."
   }
  ]
 },
 "C03": {
  "gate": "G1",
  "title": "Match release identity to running state",
  "lead_role": "infrastructure",
  "requirement": "Freeze the artifacts that define identity for each release. Check the running setup against the approved manifest, and emit the matching agent-type-id. Include the digest and signature of weights and adapters you host yourself. Test that a changed prompt, model, weights file, or tool setup can't keep showing the old approved identity without being caught.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Manifest missing helper models, budgets, or other components",
    "owner": "C01"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Hosted model changing behind a fixed name",
    "owner": "C05"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Agent-type-id not computed as a hash of its identity inputs",
    "owner": "A03"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Container image not signature-checked at admission",
    "owner": "B10"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "C03.1",
    "check": "Identity-defining artifacts are referenced in deployment config by digest or a version that can't change, not by mutable tags.",
    "verify": "1) Open deployment config: Kubernetes manifests, Helm values, Terraform, compose files. 2) Check images use `@sha256:` and prompts, models, and weights use fixed versions. 3) Follow variables and defaults to the final value. 4) Answer no if any reference is a tag like `:latest`, a branch, a model alias, or a default that falls back to one."
   },
   {
    "id": "C03.2",
    "check": "A process outside the agent observes the running artifacts (image digest, prompt, model, tool setup) and compares them with the approved manifest, rather than trusting what the agent reports.",
    "verify": "1) Find a process outside the agent (admission controller, CI check, sidecar, scheduled job) that reads running artifacts. 2) Check it observes image digest, prompt, model, and tool setup itself and compares each with the approved manifest. 3) Answer no if it trusts values the agent reports, or compares a value with itself. Answer unknown if it would run in a platform outside this repo."
   },
   {
    "id": "C03.3",
    "check": "The emitted agent-type-id is computed from the observed running artifacts, so a changed prompt, model, weights file, or tool setup produces a different ID instead of the approved one.",
    "verify": "1) Find where the emitted `agent.type.id` comes from. 2) Check it is computed from the observed running artifacts at start or deploy. 3) Answer no if it is a constant, an env var or config value set at build, or copied from the approved manifest, so a changed prompt, model, weights file, or tool setup would still emit the approved ID."
   },
   {
    "id": "C03.4",
    "check": "Self-hosted weights and adapters are checked by digest and signature at load or deploy.",
    "verify": "1) Find where self-hosted weights and adapters are loaded or deployed (`from_pretrained`, `torch.load`, `safetensors`, download steps). 2) Check a digest compare and a signature check run before use. 3) Answer no if either is missing, wrapped in `try/except` that continues, or a second load path skips it. If only hosted models are used, answer yes citing the config that shows that."
   },
   {
    "id": "C03.5",
    "check": "A mismatch blocks or quarantines the deployment, not only logs it.",
    "verify": "1) Find the code that handles a manifest or digest mismatch. 2) Check it blocks or quarantines: non-zero exit, admission deny, refusal to start, scale to zero. 3) Answer no if the mismatch branch only logs, warns, or emits a metric and continues, or an error in the check lets deployment proceed."
   }
  ]
 },
 "C04": {
  "gate": "G2",
  "title": "Detect drift per agent",
  "lead_role": "operations",
  "requirement": "Compare running images, harness settings, prompts, MCP server lists, permissions, network policies, and self-hosted model weights against their approved baseline. Simulate a prompt edit, an added tool, and a looser egress rule. Check that each one raises an alert and gets the documented response \u2014 block, quarantine, or rollback \u2014 within a set time.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Hosted model changing behind a fixed name",
    "owner": "C05"
   },
   {
    "problem": "A tool server changing its description or schema after approval",
    "owner": "E03"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Who may change production config, and emergency change records",
    "owner": "C02"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Manifest missing components that define the release",
    "owner": "C01"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "C04.1",
    "check": "A drift check compares running images, harness settings, prompts, MCP server lists, permissions, network policies, and self-hosted model weights against the approved baseline, with each of these covered.",
    "verify": "1) Find the drift check job or tool. 2) List what it compares: images, harness settings, prompts, MCP server lists, permissions, network policies, self-hosted weights. 3) Check each is compared with the approved baseline, not with the live state itself. 4) Answer no if any category is missing or excluded. Answer unknown if drift detection runs in an outside platform."
   },
   {
    "id": "C04.2",
    "check": "The drift check runs per agent deployment, on a schedule or on change events.",
    "verify": "1) Find what triggers the drift check: cron or CI schedule, admission webhook, or change events. 2) Check it runs for each agent deployment, not once for the fleet or only by hand. 3) Answer no if it is manual-only or covers only some deployments. Answer unknown if scheduling is set outside this repo."
   },
   {
    "id": "C04.3",
    "check": "Detected drift triggers an alert and a set response (block, quarantine, or rollback) defined in config, with a time limit.",
    "verify": "1) Open the drift check's result handling. 2) Check drift raises an alert and triggers a response set in config: block, quarantine, or rollback. 3) Check a time limit is set. 4) Answer no if drift only logs, the response or time limit is missing, or a check error is treated as no drift."
   }
  ]
 },
 "C05": {
  "gate": "G2",
  "title": "Control dependency, model, and training changes",
  "lead_role": "agent_team",
  "requirement": "Pin dependency versions. Where supported, also pin each model's base version, checkpoint or weights, and fine-tune or adapter version. Before promotion, link any changed training run, dataset, or training code or config to the model it produced and to its test results. Record the model version actually served, when the runtime or provider shares it, separately from the name you requested. Before loading weights, adapters, or datasets you host, check their signature against an approved signer (for example, with OpenSSF Model Signing). Also check their file format. Pickle-based formats can run code when loaded, so they need an approved exception and a scan. Test swapping a model, checkpoint, or adapter, and check that it triggers review and re-testing. Some remote services or names can change behind a fixed name. For those, record missing training and version details clearly. Watch available version metadata and provider notices, and decide when to re-test or stop using the model. A local config hash alone can't prove remote behavior is unchanged. A pinned snapshot fixes the weights, not the provider's serving systems, which can change under the same ID. Keep scheduled re-testing.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Tool or MCP server approved without reviewing publisher, permissions, or vulnerabilities",
    "owner": "E02"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Container image not pinned by digest or signature-checked",
    "owner": "B10"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Rollback setup naming a hosted model that is retired",
    "owner": "C07"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "C05.1",
    "check": "Dependency versions are pinned with exact versions or hashes in lockfiles, with no ranges or floating tags.",
    "verify": "1) Open every dependency file and lockfile (`requirements*.txt`, `poetry.lock`, `package-lock.json`, `go.sum`) plus Dockerfile `FROM` and install lines. 2) Look for ranges (`^`, `~`, `>=`, `*`), missing lockfiles, and floating tags. 3) Answer no if any dependency is unpinned, including install commands in Dockerfiles or CI scripts that bypass the lockfile."
   },
   {
    "id": "C05.2",
    "check": "Model IDs in config are pinned snapshots (plus checkpoint, weights, and adapter versions where supported), not aliases such as 'latest'.",
    "verify": "1) Find every model ID in config, env files, and code defaults. 2) Follow env vars to their fallback value. 3) Answer no if any is an alias (`-latest`, undated name), falls back to one when unset, or leaves out the checkpoint, weights, or adapter version where one is used and the provider supports pinning it."
   },
   {
    "id": "C05.3",
    "check": "Self-hosted weights, adapters, and datasets have their signatures checked against an approved signer before loading, and loading stops if the check fails.",
    "verify": "1) Find load paths for self-hosted weights, adapters, and datasets. 2) Check a signature check against an approved signer (for example OpenSSF Model Signing, `cosign verify` with a pinned identity) runs before loading. 3) Answer no if any path skips it, any signer is accepted, or a failure is caught and loading continues. If nothing is self-hosted, answer yes citing the config that shows that."
   },
   {
    "id": "C05.4",
    "check": "Pickle-based model formats are refused at load unless an approved exception and scan are recorded.",
    "verify": "1) Search for pickle-capable loads: `pickle.load`, `torch.load`, `joblib.load`, `.pkl`, `.pt`, `.bin` files. 2) Check the loader refuses these formats unless an approved exception and scan are recorded. 3) Answer no if any pickle-based load runs without that check."
   },
   {
    "id": "C05.5",
    "check": "The model version the provider or runtime reports serving is recorded separately from the requested model name.",
    "verify": "1) Find model call sites and their response handling. 2) Check the served version from the response or runtime (for example the response `model` field) is stored in its own field. 3) Answer no if only the requested name is recorded, or the served field is filled with the requested value."
   }
  ]
 },
 "C06": {
  "gate": "G2",
  "title": "Re-test changed behavior",
  "lead_role": "agent_team",
  "requirement": "Before promotion, run typical allowed tasks and attack cases against the release candidate. Include destructive-action denial, access limits, injection containment, audit attribution, and stopping. Include retrieval, memory, and checker tests where they apply. If misalignment is in scope, include attempts to slip past monitoring and the stop path. Keep the results for the exact candidate manifest.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Running config drifting from the approved baseline unnoticed",
    "owner": "C04"
   },
   {
    "problem": "No tested rollback to an approved setup",
    "owner": "C07"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Config changes made without a reviewed diff and named author",
    "owner": "C02"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "C06.1",
    "check": "A CI release gate runs the test suite against the exact candidate (manifest digest and model IDs), with pass criteria set before the run, and blocks promotion on any blocking failure.",
    "verify": "1) Open the CI workflow for release or promotion. 2) Check it runs the suite against the candidate's manifest digest and model IDs, with pass criteria in config before the run. 3) Check promotion depends on it. 4) Answer no if the job uses `continue-on-error`, `|| true`, or allowed failures, tests a different build, or promotion can run without it."
   },
   {
    "id": "C06.2",
    "check": "The suite includes cases for destructive-action denial, access limits, injection containment, audit attribution, and stopping, plus retrieval, memory, and checker tests where these apply.",
    "verify": "1) List the test cases in the release suite. 2) Check each category exists: destructive-action denial, access limits, injection containment, audit attribution, stopping, plus retrieval, memory, and checker tests where those features exist. 3) Answer no if any category is missing or its tests are skipped (`skip`, `xfail`, excluded markers)."
   },
   {
    "id": "C06.3",
    "check": "Test results are stored linked to the exact candidate manifest.",
    "verify": "1) Find where CI stores test results (artifacts, uploads, database). 2) Check each result is tagged with the candidate's manifest digest or type ID. 3) Answer no if results stay only in CI logs, are tagged only by branch or build number, or are not stored."
   }
  ]
 },
 "C07": {
  "gate": "G2",
  "title": "Rollback and restart",
  "lead_role": "operations",
  "requirement": "Keep an approved recovery setup and show that rollback works. Confirm each hosted model it names is still offered, and record the announced retirement date. Account for changed credentials, memory and index state, checkpoints, and queued work. Don't restore revoked permissions or replay unsafe side effects just because the old artifact still exists.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Changed or tampered checkpoints accepted, or unsafe replay of actions",
    "owner": "R14"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Hosted model changing behind a fixed name",
    "owner": "C05"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Stop doesn't halt running work or open tool streams within a set time",
    "owner": "R11"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "C07.1",
    "check": "An approved recovery setup is defined with references that can't change, and a rollback procedure or pipeline exists in code.",
    "verify": "1) Find the recovery setup: a pinned earlier manifest or release record. 2) Check its references are digests or fixed versions. 3) Find a rollback script, pipeline, or runbook in the repo. 4) Answer no if references are tags or aliases, or no rollback procedure exists."
   },
   {
    "id": "C07.2",
    "check": "Credentials and permission grants are managed apart from the rolled-back artifacts, so a rollback does not restore revoked access.",
    "verify": "1) Open the rollback procedure. 2) Check what it restores. 3) Answer no if it re-applies IAM policies, RoleBindings, secrets, or API keys from the old release, so revoked access comes back. Answer unknown if credentials are managed in another system and the repo does not show how."
   },
   {
    "id": "C07.3",
    "check": "The rollback procedure handles memory and index state, checkpoints, and queued work explicitly (drain, migrate, or discard), so unsafe side effects are not replayed.",
    "verify": "1) Open the rollback procedure. 2) Look for explicit steps for memory and index state, checkpoints, and queued work: drain, migrate, or discard. 3) Answer no if any of these is not handled, or queued work is replayed as-is."
   },
   {
    "id": "C07.4",
    "check": "Each hosted model named in the recovery setup has a recorded retirement date.",
    "verify": "1) List the hosted models named in the recovery setup. 2) Check each has a recorded retirement date. 3) Answer no if any hosted model lacks one.",
    "doc_ok": true
   }
  ]
 },
 "C08": {
  "gate": "G2",
  "title": "Connect configuration to execution",
  "lead_role": "operations",
  "requirement": "From a trace, look up the release manifest, the context sizes at each decision, and any prompts a parent passed to a sub-agent. Keep these links for the full audit retention period. Control access to sensitive artifacts.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Context size not measured or logged for each action decision",
    "owner": "R07"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Action records missing any of the six identity fields",
    "owner": "A02"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Manifest missing components that define the release",
    "owner": "C01"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "C08.1",
    "check": "Each trace or audit record carries the agent-type-id or manifest reference that resolves to the stored release manifest.",
    "verify": "1) Open the trace or audit record writer. 2) Check every record carries `agent.type.id` or a manifest reference. 3) Check that value is the key of a stored manifest. 4) Answer no if the field is missing on some paths (errors, background jobs) or holds a value that resolves to no stored manifest."
   },
   {
    "id": "C08.2",
    "check": "The context size at each model decision is recorded in the trace.",
    "verify": "1) Find model call sites and their spans. 2) Look for the context size recorded per call (for example `gen_ai.usage.input_tokens` or a context-length attribute). 3) Answer no if it is missing on any model call path, including retries and sub-agents."
   },
   {
    "id": "C08.3",
    "check": "Prompts passed from a parent to a sub-agent are stored and linked from the trace.",
    "verify": "1) Find sub-agent spawn code. 2) Check the parent's prompt is stored and the trace holds a link to it (ID, hash, or URI). 3) Answer no if the prompt is not stored, prompt capture is off, or the trace has no link."
   },
   {
    "id": "C08.4",
    "check": "Storage config keeps manifests, traces, and these links for at least the audit retention period, and limits access to sensitive artifacts by role.",
    "verify": "1) Find storage config for manifests, traces, and prompt links: bucket lifecycle rules, log retention, TTLs. 2) Check retention covers the audit period for all of them. 3) Check IAM limits sensitive artifacts by role. 4) Answer no if any has a shorter TTL, no retention, or broad read access. Answer unknown if storage is configured outside this repo."
   }
  ]
 },
 "E01": {
  "gate": "G2",
  "title": "Map shared responsibilities",
  "lead_role": "governance",
  "requirement": "List the outside tools, MCP servers, peer agents, sub-agents, registries, identity services, gateways, stop channels, and audit services. For each control that applies, name the owner on the agent team and the owner on the shared platform or vendor side. Keep evidence for both halves. Using an upstream service doesn't make its controls N/A.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "A tool call that skips the MCP gateway or reaches a tool server directly",
    "owner": "E06"
   },
   {
    "problem": "A vendor service dropping agent or tenant identity from its records",
    "owner": "E10"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Tool or MCP server approved without vetting or pinning",
    "owner": "E02"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "E01.1",
    "check": "An inventory file lists the outside tools, MCP servers, peer agents, sub-agents, registries, identity services, gateways, stop channels, and audit services.",
    "verify": "1) Find the inventory file. 2) Check each category the agent uses is listed: outside tools, MCP servers, peer agents, sub-agents, registries, identity services, gateways, stop channels, audit services. 3) Answer no if no inventory exists or a category the agent uses is missing.",
    "doc_ok": true
   },
   {
    "id": "E01.2",
    "check": "Each inventory entry names both an agent-team owner and a platform or vendor owner.",
    "verify": "1) Open each inventory entry. 2) Check it names an agent-team owner and a platform or vendor owner. 3) Answer no if any entry is missing either owner.",
    "doc_ok": true
   },
   {
    "id": "E01.3",
    "check": "Every outside service the code or config calls appears in the inventory.",
    "verify": "1) Search code and config for outside calls: HTTP clients and base URLs, SDK clients, `*.mcp.json` servers, queue and webhook endpoints, identity providers, telemetry exporters. 2) Compare each with the inventory. 3) Answer no if any called service is not listed.",
    "doc_ok": true
   }
  ]
 },
 "E02": {
  "gate": "G2",
  "title": "Vet and pin dependencies",
  "lead_role": "security",
  "requirement": "Before approving a tool or server, review its source code or available provenance, publisher, permissions, update path, and known vulnerabilities. Include model files, adapters, and datasets. Pin versions or digests, and verify signatures where they exist. A registry's namespace check shows who controls a publisher account, not that the code is safe. Record the limits of remote services and unsigned artifacts, and the extra checks you use instead.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved",
    "owner": "E03"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Hidden instructions inside a tool's metadata",
    "owner": "E04"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Lookalike or duplicate tool names across servers",
    "owner": "E05"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Container image not pinned by digest or signature-checked",
    "owner": "B10"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "E02.1",
    "check": "Each tool, MCP server, package, model file, adapter, and dataset is pinned by exact version or digest, with no floating tags, ranges, or 'latest'.",
    "verify": "1) Open lockfiles, `*.mcp.json`, Dockerfiles, and model or dataset download code. 2) Check each tool, MCP server, package, model file, adapter, and dataset uses an exact version or digest. 3) Answer no if any uses a range, `latest`, a branch, `npx` or `uvx` without a version, or an unpinned download URL."
   },
   {
    "id": "E02.2",
    "check": "Signature checks run in the build or load path where the publisher provides signatures, and a failed check stops the install or load.",
    "verify": "1) Find install and load steps in CI and code. 2) Check signature checks (`cosign verify`, `npm audit signatures`, sigstore, GPG) run where the publisher signs. 3) Answer no if a check is missing, its failure is ignored (`|| true`, `continue-on-error`, `try/except` that continues), or a second install path skips it."
   },
   {
    "id": "E02.3",
    "check": "A vulnerability scan of the pinned dependency list runs in CI and gates on the team's release policy.",
    "verify": "1) Open CI workflows. 2) Find a vulnerability scan (`pip-audit`, `npm audit`, `osv-scanner`, `trivy`, `grype`) of the pinned dependency list. 3) Check it fails the build per the release policy. 4) Answer no if no scan exists, it is set not to fail (`--exit-zero`, `|| true`, `continue-on-error`), or it scans a different file than the lockfile actually used."
   },
   {
    "id": "E02.4",
    "check": "Each approved dependency's record names its publisher, permissions, and update source, and remote services or unsigned artifacts have a recorded exception.",
    "verify": "1) Find the dependency approval records. 2) Check each names publisher, permissions, and update source. 3) Check each remote service and unsigned artifact has a recorded exception. 4) Answer no if any approved dependency lacks these fields or the needed exception.",
    "doc_ok": true
   }
  ]
 },
 "E03": {
  "gate": "G2",
  "title": "Re-check tools on load",
  "lead_role": "infrastructure",
  "requirement": "Take a fingerprint of each approved tool's description and schema. Compare it every time the tool loads, the cache refreshes, or the server says its tool list changed. Don't use a cached definition when the re-check fails. Test a changed description, schema, or implementation version, and block unexpected changes until they are reviewed. A fingerprint of the description and schema catches changes to that metadata, not hidden changes to remote code. Pin or attest the implementation where you can see it. For remote implementations, record what you can't inspect and when you'll review them again. Track vulnerability notices, and find which deployments use an affected dependency.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Hidden instructions in a tool's metadata when first approved",
    "owner": "E04"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Tool approved without reviewing its publisher and permissions",
    "owner": "E02"
   },
   {
    "problem": "A new server quietly swapping its tool in for an approved one",
    "owner": "E05"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Cached tool list shared across authorization contexts",
    "owner": "E06"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "The agent loads no external tools or MCP servers; all tools are built in and fixed in the release.",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "E03.1",
    "check": "A fingerprint of each approved tool's description and schema is stored.",
    "verify": "1) Find where tool definitions are loaded (MCP `tools/list`, tool registry). 2) Look for a stored hash of each approved tool's description and schema. 3) Answer no if no fingerprint is stored, or it covers only the name and not the description and schema."
   },
   {
    "id": "E03.2",
    "check": "The fingerprint is re-checked on every tool load, cache refresh, and list-changed notice from the server.",
    "verify": "1) List every path that loads tool definitions: startup, cache refresh, reconnect, and the `notifications/tools/list_changed` handler. 2) Check each recomputes the fingerprint and compares it with the stored one. 3) Answer no if any path skips the compare, or both sides of the compare are computed from the new definition."
   },
   {
    "id": "E03.3",
    "check": "When the re-check fails, the tool is blocked and the cached definition is not used.",
    "verify": "1) Open the fingerprint mismatch branch. 2) Check it blocks the tool and does not fall back to the cached definition. 3) Answer no if it only logs, uses the cached or new definition anyway, or an error during the check lets the tool load."
   },
   {
    "id": "E03.4",
    "check": "The implementation version is pinned or attested where it can be seen, and recorded separately from the metadata fingerprint.",
    "verify": "1) Check how each tool server implementation is referenced: package version, image digest, or attestation. 2) Check that version is recorded in its own field, apart from the metadata fingerprint. 3) Answer no if a visible implementation is unpinned, or only the metadata hash is recorded."
   }
  ]
 },
 "E04": {
  "gate": "G2",
  "title": "Inspect tool metadata",
  "lead_role": "security",
  "requirement": "Before the model sees them, scan server instructions, tool names, titles, descriptions, input and output schemas, annotations, and prompt and resource templates. Look for hidden instructions, references to other servers' tools, invisible or control characters, role overrides, encoded payloads, and places data could be sent. Limit lengths, and clean or reject anything suspicious. Check that a scanner error doesn't quietly let unchecked metadata through.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved",
    "owner": "E03"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Hidden instructions in tool call results",
    "owner": "R01"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Lookalike or duplicate tool names across servers",
    "owner": "E05"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Allow decisions based on server hints such as read-only",
    "owner": "B01"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "The agent loads no external tools or MCP servers; all tool metadata is written in-house and fixed in the release.",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "E04.1",
    "check": "A scanner runs on server instructions, tool names, titles, descriptions, input and output schemas, annotations, and prompt and resource templates before the model sees them.",
    "verify": "1) Find where server instructions and tool metadata reach the model's context. 2) Check a scanner runs before that on every field: server instructions, names, titles, descriptions, input and output schemas, annotations, prompt and resource templates. 3) Answer no if any field, or a second load path, skips the scanner."
   },
   {
    "id": "E04.2",
    "check": "The scanner checks for hidden instructions, references to other servers' tools, invisible or control characters, role overrides, encoded payloads, and data-sending targets, and enforces length limits.",
    "verify": "1) Open the scanner rules. 2) Check for each: hidden instructions, references to other servers' tools, invisible or control characters (zero-width, bidi), role overrides, encoded payloads (base64), data-sending targets (URLs, emails), and length limits. 3) Answer no if any one is missing."
   },
   {
    "id": "E04.3",
    "check": "Metadata the scanner flags is cleaned or rejected, not just logged.",
    "verify": "1) Open the scanner's result handling. 2) Check flagged metadata is cleaned, or the tool rejected, before the model sees it. 3) Answer no if flagged items are only logged or warned about and passed through."
   },
   {
    "id": "E04.4",
    "check": "A scanner error or outage blocks the metadata instead of letting it through unchecked.",
    "verify": "1) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, and timeout around the scanner call. 2) Check an error blocks the metadata. 3) Answer no if any error branch passes metadata through, returns clean, or skips the scan, or a config flag disables scanning by default."
   }
  ]
 },
 "E05": {
  "gate": "G2",
  "title": "Verified, namespaced tools",
  "lead_role": "infrastructure",
  "requirement": "Identify each tool by a verified server identity plus the tool name. Base server identity on the verified endpoint, credential, or registry namespace. A server's self-reported name is neither unique nor trustworthy. Flag lookalike and duplicate names across servers. Test that a new server can't quietly swap its tool in for an approved one.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Hidden instructions inside a tool's metadata",
    "owner": "E04"
   },
   {
    "problem": "A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved",
    "owner": "E03"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Peer agent identity not authenticated on each message",
    "owner": "E07"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Gateway not authenticating callers or authorizing each action",
    "owner": "E06"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "The agent loads no external tools or MCP servers.",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "E05.1",
    "check": "Tools are keyed by verified server identity (endpoint, credential, or registry namespace) plus tool name, never by bare tool name or a server's self-reported name.",
    "verify": "1) Find the tool registry or lookup map. 2) Check its key is a verified endpoint, credential, or registry namespace plus tool name. 3) Answer no if tools are keyed by bare tool name or the server's self-reported name (`serverInfo.name`), or a later server overwrites an earlier entry with the same tool name."
   },
   {
    "id": "E05.2",
    "check": "The registry flags duplicate and lookalike tool names across servers.",
    "verify": "1) Open tool registration code. 2) Look for a check that compares new tool names across servers for duplicates and lookalikes (case, homoglyphs, separators). 3) Answer no if registration silently overwrites or accepts duplicates."
   },
   {
    "id": "E05.3",
    "check": "Approval is bound to the verified server identity, so a new server with a matching tool name or self-reported name does not inherit approval or replace the approved tool.",
    "verify": "1) Find where tool approval is stored and checked. 2) Check approval is keyed by verified server identity, not tool name or self-reported server name. 3) Answer no if a new server with a matching tool name or server name would count as approved or replace the approved tool."
   }
  ]
 },
 "E06": {
  "gate": "G2",
  "title": "Enforced gateway boundary",
  "lead_role": "infrastructure",
  "requirement": "Send MCP calls through a gateway, or a similar enforcement layer, that the agent can't get around. Authenticate each caller (prove who they are), authorize each action, and validate calls and responses. Enforce rate limits and log every decision. For HTTP and OAuth, check the token's issuer, intended audience, expiry, and scopes. Use properly authorized credentials for the services behind the gateway. Never pass an unchecked token through. For local tools that talk over standard input/output (stdio), control process launch, file access, credentials, and networking in the host or sandbox. An HTTP gateway alone doesn't cover these. Test going around the gateway straight to the server, bad credentials, broken responses, and outages of the policy engine or scanner. MCP copies some request details into headers. If routing or policy uses those headers, reject any request whose headers don't match its body, before checking policy. Store any cached tool list or response separately for each authorization context. When the gateway is itself an OAuth client, check the issuer (`iss`, RFC 9207). Also protect discovery URLs against SSRF (tricking a server into calling places it shouldn't). Required checks must fail closed: if a required check can't run, block the action.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Tool inventory missing a task-based reason for each tool",
    "owner": "B01"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Agent credential granting wildcard or admin permissions",
    "owner": "B02"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Destructive actions running without a higher-authority approval",
    "owner": "B05"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Hidden instructions inside a tool's metadata",
    "owner": "E04"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Outbound traffic to unapproved internet destinations",
    "owner": "B09"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "The agent uses no MCP servers or similar external tool servers.",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "E06.1",
    "check": "The agent can reach MCP servers only through the gateway: network policy blocks direct routes, the agent holds no server credentials, and any local stdio servers are launched by the host or sandbox with limited files, credentials, and network.",
    "verify": "1) Find how the agent reaches MCP servers: URLs in `*.mcp.json` and client config. 2) Check all go to the gateway, and NetworkPolicy or egress rules block direct routes. 3) Check the agent holds no server credentials and stdio servers run in a restricted sandbox. 4) Answer no if any tool call bypasses the gateway. Answer unknown if network policy lives outside this repo."
   },
   {
    "id": "E06.2",
    "check": "The gateway checks each token's issuer, audience, expiry, and scopes, and uses its own separately authorized credentials downstream instead of passing the caller's token through.",
    "verify": "1) Open the gateway's token validation. 2) Check issuer, audience, expiry, and scopes are each verified. 3) Check downstream calls use the gateway's own credentials. 4) Answer no if any claim is unchecked, signature verification is disabled, or the caller's `Authorization` header is forwarded downstream."
   },
   {
    "id": "E06.3",
    "check": "The gateway authorizes each action, validates requests and responses, enforces rate limits, and logs every decision.",
    "verify": "1) Open the gateway's request handling. 2) Check per-action authorization, request and response validation, rate limits, and a log entry for every decision. 3) Answer no if any is missing, authorization happens only once per connection, or denials are not logged."
   },
   {
    "id": "E06.4",
    "check": "If a required check (policy engine, scanner, token check) errors or times out, the call is blocked.",
    "verify": "1) Read every `try/except`, `catch`, and timeout around the policy engine, scanner, and token check. 2) Check each error blocks the call. 3) Answer no if any error branch allows the call or logs and continues, or a config flag defaults to fail-open."
   },
   {
    "id": "E06.5",
    "check": "Requests whose routing headers (such as Mcp-Name) don't match the body are rejected before policy, and cached tool lists and responses are stored per authorization context.",
    "verify": "1) Find where routing or policy reads headers such as `Mcp-Name`. 2) Check the header is compared with the body before policy runs, and mismatches are rejected. 3) Check tool-list and response caches are keyed by authorization context. 4) Answer no if headers are trusted without the compare, or a cache is shared across callers."
   },
   {
    "id": "E06.6",
    "check": "If the gateway is itself an OAuth client, it checks the authorization server's issuer (iss, RFC 9207) and blocks private and metadata addresses in discovery URLs. Answer yes with the code line, or yes citing the config that shows it isn't an OAuth client.",
    "verify": "1) Check whether the gateway acts as an OAuth client (gets tokens, follows discovery or metadata URLs). 2) If so, check it verifies the `iss` parameter (RFC 9207) and blocks private, loopback, and metadata addresses (169.254.169.254) in discovery URLs. 3) Answer no if either is missing. If it is not an OAuth client, answer yes citing the config that shows that."
   }
  ]
 },
 "E07": {
  "gate": "G2",
  "title": "Untrusted peer messages",
  "lead_role": "agent_team",
  "requirement": "Authenticate the sending agent (prove its identity) on every message, even validly signed ones. Validate message types and schemas, and check what that peer is allowed to ask for. Where a replay could repeat an action, check freshness and track nonces or request IDs. Checking the sender alone doesn't stop replays. Test faked identity, replayed requests, requests across tenants, and instructions passed along by a compromised peer. Verify signed agent cards where they exist. A card says who an agent is, not what it may do. Check callback and webhook URLs against SSRF. A valid sender doesn't make a message's content trusted.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Tool server identified only by its self-reported name",
    "owner": "E05"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Missing schema or size checks on API responses",
    "owner": "R01"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Peers or children still running after a stop",
    "owner": "E09"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Run can't be rebuilt across gateway, tool services, and delegated workers",
    "owner": "E10"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "The agent receives no messages from other agents (no A2A server, peer endpoint, or agent-to-agent channel).",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "E07.1",
    "check": "Every peer message is authenticated to a specific sending agent, even when the message is validly signed.",
    "verify": "1) Find the handler for incoming peer messages (A2A server, peer endpoint). 2) Check each message is authenticated to a specific sending agent (mTLS identity, verified token subject). 3) Answer no if any route accepts unauthenticated messages, trusts a self-declared sender field, or accepts any valid signature without mapping it to a known agent."
   },
   {
    "id": "E07.2",
    "check": "Message types and schemas are validated, and each request is checked against what that peer is allowed to ask for, including tenant.",
    "verify": "1) Open the peer message handler. 2) Check message type and schema validation. 3) Check a per-peer allowlist of request types and tenants, keyed on the authenticated sender. 4) Answer no if schemas are unchecked, any authenticated peer can ask for anything, or tenant is taken from the message body."
   },
   {
    "id": "E07.3",
    "check": "Action requests carry a nonce or request ID and a freshness check, and replays are rejected or do not repeat side effects.",
    "verify": "1) Find action request handling. 2) Check for a nonce or request ID plus a freshness (timestamp) check, with seen IDs stored. 3) Answer no if no replay check exists, the nonce is never stored or compared, or a repeated ID repeats side effects."
   },
   {
    "id": "E07.4",
    "check": "Peer message content is handled as untrusted data and is not placed into system or instruction prompts.",
    "verify": "1) Trace peer message content to the prompt builder. 2) Check it goes into user or tool-result content as untrusted data. 3) Answer no if any path places it into the system prompt or an instruction template."
   },
   {
    "id": "E07.5",
    "check": "Callback and webhook URLs are checked against SSRF, with private and metadata addresses blocked.",
    "verify": "1) Find code that calls callback or webhook URLs from peers. 2) Check URL validation blocks private, loopback, link-local, and metadata addresses (169.254.169.254). 3) Answer no if any callback is fetched without the check, or only the hostname text is checked and not the resolved address."
   }
  ]
 },
 "E08": {
  "gate": "G1",
  "title": "Bounded delegated authority",
  "lead_role": "agent_team",
  "requirement": "Give sub-agents only the powers approved for their task. Enforce each child's role and any explicit approval from a higher authority. A parent must not gain a power it was denied just by asking a more powerful peer to act. Narrow credentials at each hop, for example with token exchange or transaction tokens. Authorize based on the original subject (the user or system whose authority is used) and the current actor. Earlier actors in a delegation chain are for the record only, not a reason to grant access. Test that escalation attempt.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Approvals that can be replayed or aren't tied to exact arguments",
    "owner": "B06"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded",
    "owner": "A06"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Children still running after the parent is stopped",
    "owner": "E09"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Replayed or faked peer-agent messages",
    "owner": "E07"
   },
   {
    "problem": "No limit on how many sub-agents a run can start",
    "owner": "B07"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "The agent never delegates to sub-agents or peer agents.",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "E08.1",
    "check": "Each child gets a credential narrowed to its approved task (for example by token exchange or transaction tokens), never the parent's full credential.",
    "verify": "1) Find where a child agent gets its credential. 2) Check it is a narrowed token for the task (token exchange, transaction token, scoped-down role). 3) Answer no if any spawn path passes the parent's token, API key, or full role, or the child inherits the parent's environment credentials."
   },
   {
    "id": "E08.2",
    "check": "Authorization at the action uses the original subject and the current actor, and earlier actors in the delegation chain never add access.",
    "verify": "1) Open authorization code for delegated actions. 2) Check it decides on the original subject and the current actor. 3) Answer no if it grants access because of any earlier actor in the chain (nested `act` claims, parent identity) or a union of chain permissions."
   },
   {
    "id": "E08.3",
    "check": "An action the parent was denied is also denied when routed through a more powerful peer, unless it carries its own explicit approval.",
    "verify": "1) Find how a parent asks a peer or child to act. 2) Check the request carries the parent's limits and the peer re-checks them, or requires its own explicit approval. 3) Answer no if a peer acts with its own wider permissions on a parent's request without that check."
   },
   {
    "id": "E08.4",
    "check": "Downstream services reject the parent's token when the child presents it (audience-bound tokens).",
    "verify": "1) Find how child tokens are issued and how downstream services validate tokens. 2) Check tokens are audience-bound and services verify `aud`. 3) Answer no if audience is unset, a wildcard, or not checked. Answer unknown if downstream services are outside this repo."
   }
  ]
 },
 "E09": {
  "gate": "G1",
  "title": "Recursive shutdown",
  "lead_role": "operations",
  "requirement": "Run a drill across a parent, nested children, remote workers, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks, where they exist. Check that the stop reaches every descendant within the set time. It must revoke access as needed and prevent orphaned retries or restarts. Record how outside effects that already happened are reconciled.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Agent able to disable or delay its own stop",
    "owner": "R11"
   },
   {
    "problem": "No way to cut off the agent's access without cutting off the user",
    "owner": "B04"
   },
   {
    "problem": "No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type",
    "owner": "E12"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Sub-agent given powers beyond its task",
    "owner": "E08"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "E09.1",
    "check": "A stop signal from the root reaches every descendant: nested children, remote workers, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks.",
    "verify": "1) Find the stop handler. 2) Check it reaches nested children, remote workers, queued jobs, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks (cancel calls, MCP `notifications/cancelled`). 3) Answer no if any of these that exists in the code is not reached, or stop only sets an in-process flag that remote workers never read."
   },
   {
    "id": "E09.2",
    "check": "Stopping revokes descendants' credentials or leases, and leases or tokens are short-lived, so a disconnected worker loses authority within the stop time limit.",
    "verify": "1) Open the stop path and credential issuing code. 2) Check stopping revokes descendants' tokens or leases. 3) Check token or lease lifetime fits within the stop time limit. 4) Answer no if no revocation exists, or tokens are long-lived so a disconnected worker keeps authority."
   },
   {
    "id": "E09.3",
    "check": "Queues and schedulers refuse new work, retries, and restarts for a stopped tree.",
    "verify": "1) Find queue consumers, schedulers, and retry logic. 2) Check each looks up the stopped state before running new work, retries, or restarts. 3) Answer no if any consumer, retry decorator, or restart policy runs without that check."
   },
   {
    "id": "E09.4",
    "check": "Outside effects already completed are recorded for reconciliation.",
    "verify": "1) Open the stop path. 2) Check it records outside effects already completed (tool calls, writes, sent messages) for reconciliation. 3) Answer no if nothing is recorded, or only in-flight work is listed."
   }
  ]
 },
 "E10": {
  "gate": "G1",
  "title": "Audit across service boundaries",
  "lead_role": "infrastructure",
  "requirement": "Rebuild a run across the gateway, tool services, and delegated workers, with all six identity fields kept. Check that the agent can't erase or rewrite its audit history, either directly or by asking a more powerful tool or peer. Check that a vendor boundary doesn't leave any action untraceable to the agent and instance that did it.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Audit retention, access, redaction, or export rules missing",
    "owner": "E11"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Authorization decisions missing the policy version in the run record",
    "owner": "R13"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Instance or tenant labels taken from what the model supplies",
    "owner": "A04"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Recovery checkpoints not tamper-evident",
    "owner": "R14"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "E10.1",
    "check": "The gateway, tool services, and delegated workers each write audit records with all six identity fields and the shared trace context.",
    "verify": "1) Find audit writers in the gateway, tool services, and delegated workers. 2) Check each writes all six identity fields and the shared trace context. 3) Answer no if any component writes records without them or starts a new trace. Answer unknown if a component's code is outside this repo."
   },
   {
    "id": "E10.2",
    "check": "The agent's credentials have no permission to delete, update, or overwrite audit records (for example an append-only or WORM store with IAM denies).",
    "verify": "1) Find the agent's IAM role or database grants and the audit store config. 2) Check the agent has no delete, update, or overwrite rights (`DeleteObject`, overwriting `PutObject`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, `TRUNCATE`) and the store is append-only or WORM (Object Lock). 3) Answer no if the agent's credentials can delete or rewrite records, or grant `*`. Answer unknown if these are set outside this repo."
   },
   {
    "id": "E10.3",
    "check": "No tool or peer the agent can call is able to delete or rewrite audit records on its behalf, or such requests are held for approval under B05.",
    "verify": "1) List the tools and peers the agent can call. 2) For each, check whether it can delete or rewrite audit records (database admin, log management, storage tools). 3) Answer no if any can and such requests are not held for approval under B05."
   },
   {
    "id": "E10.4",
    "check": "Calls into vendor services are mapped from the gateway's verified identity and correlation ID to the vendor's job or receipt ID.",
    "verify": "1) Find calls into vendor services. 2) Check the gateway stores its verified identity and correlation ID together with the vendor's job or receipt ID. 3) Answer no if the vendor ID is not stored, or stored without the link to identity and correlation ID."
   }
  ]
 },
 "E11": {
  "gate": "G2",
  "title": "Protect shared evidence",
  "lead_role": "infrastructure",
  "requirement": "Set rules for how long audit records are kept, who can see them, what is redacted, how their integrity is checked, and how they are exported and restored. Test pulling up a past run, and test that missing records or a broken pipeline get noticed. Keep records long enough for investigations and for the law. For example, the EU AI Act requires providers and deployers of high-risk systems to keep automatically generated logs for at least six months (Articles 19 and 26). High-impact actions must fail closed when required audit capture isn't working.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Agent able to erase or rewrite its own audit history",
    "owner": "E10"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Run record missing denials, approvals, or results",
    "owner": "R13"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Secrets left in tool output, responses, or normal logs",
    "owner": "R03"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "E11.1",
    "check": "Audit retention is set in config to cover the investigation window and the legal minimum (for example at least six months for EU AI Act high-risk systems).",
    "verify": "1) Find retention settings for audit storage: bucket lifecycle rules, log retention days, TTLs. 2) Check the value covers the investigation window and the legal minimum (for example six months for EU AI Act high-risk systems). 3) Answer no if retention is shorter or unset. Answer unknown if audit storage is configured outside this repo."
   },
   {
    "id": "E11.2",
    "check": "Access to audit records is limited by role, and redaction rules apply to sensitive fields.",
    "verify": "1) Find IAM policies or ACLs on audit storage. 2) Check reads are limited by role. 3) Check redaction rules apply to sensitive fields. 4) Answer no if read access is broad (`*`, all engineers) or no redaction exists."
   },
   {
    "id": "E11.3",
    "check": "Integrity protection (hash chain, signatures, or WORM) plus sequence numbers or delivery receipts let missing records be detected, with an alert on gaps or pipeline failure.",
    "verify": "1) Open the audit writer and store config. 2) Check for integrity protection (hash chain, signatures, or WORM) plus sequence numbers or delivery receipts. 3) Check an alert fires on gaps or pipeline failure. 4) Answer no if any piece is missing, or a hash is computed but never verified."
   },
   {
    "id": "E11.4",
    "check": "High-impact actions are blocked when required audit capture is down.",
    "verify": "1) Find high-impact action paths. 2) Check they confirm audit capture is working before the action runs. 3) Answer no if audit write errors are caught and the action proceeds, or the audit record is written only after success."
   }
  ]
 },
 "E12": {
  "gate": "G3",
  "title": "Fleet incident response",
  "lead_role": "security",
  "requirement": "Run a drill where a shared tool or agent type is compromised. Find the affected instances and tenants. Quarantine the dependency, revoke the access involved, stop affected work, and alert the named responders. Check that alerts about abuse across agents, and about budgets, reach the team that can act. Name who decides whether to report outside the organization and share information.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Kill switch for a single agent not drilled",
    "owner": "R11"
   },
   {
    "problem": "No owner named on each side of a shared control",
    "owner": "E01"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Tool or MCP server approved without vetting or pinning",
    "owner": "E02"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Budget and rate limits not enforced outside the model",
    "owner": "B07"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": []
 },
 "R01": {
  "gate": "G2",
  "title": "Validate every input boundary, including API responses",
  "lead_role": "agent_team",
  "requirement": "List every input the agent receives. That includes API responses (errors and streamed content too), tool and connector output, incoming webhooks and callbacks, messages from other agents, user input, fetched pages, files, and retrieved chunks. Authenticate each sender (prove who sent it), and verify signatures where they exist. Treat content as untrusted even when it comes from an authenticated service or an internal one. Before content reaches the model or runs anywhere, check its type, schema, and size. Also check its content for attack payloads and hidden prompt-injection instructions. Reject or hold aside bad payloads. If a required check fails to run, don't let unchecked content through. Test broken, oversized, faked, and hostile responses from each integration.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Injected instructions in valid content reaching forbidden tools unblocked",
    "owner": "R02"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Hidden instructions in tool names, descriptions, or schemas",
    "owner": "E04"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Replayed peer-agent requests with no freshness or nonce check",
    "owner": "E07"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Secrets leaking into outputs or normal logs",
    "owner": "R03"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Unapproved sources able to add documents to the retrieval corpus",
    "owner": "R04"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "R01.1",
    "check": "Every input path passes through validation code: API and tool responses (including errors and streams), webhooks and callbacks, agent messages, user input, fetched pages, files, and retrieved chunks.",
    "verify": "1) List every input: tool and API responses (errors, streams), webhooks, agent messages, user input, fetched pages, files, retrieved chunks. 2) Trace each to where it enters the model context. 3) Answer no if any path, including error bodies, stream chunks, or a second handler, reaches the model without passing validation code."
   },
   {
    "id": "R01.2",
    "check": "Type, schema, and size checks run before content, including streamed chunks, is added to the model context or executed.",
    "verify": "1) Find validation code (`pydantic`, `zod`, `jsonschema`, size limits, `max_bytes`, content-type checks). 2) Check it runs before content is added to the context or executed, including per stream chunk. 3) Answer no if validation runs after use, only on success responses, streams skip it, or there is no size limit."
   },
   {
    "id": "R01.3",
    "check": "Senders are authenticated, and signatures are verified where the integration provides them, including for internal and authenticated services.",
    "verify": "1) Find webhook, callback, and inbound handlers. 2) Check sender authentication and signature verification (`hmac.compare_digest`, `verify_signature`, mTLS). 3) Answer no if any handler skips it, internal services are trusted without auth, the signature is compared with `==`, or a verify failure is caught and processing continues."
   },
   {
    "id": "R01.4",
    "check": "Content is also screened for attack payloads or injection instructions, not only for structure.",
    "verify": "1) Find content screening (injection detector, pattern rules, classifier, encoded-payload checks). 2) Check it runs on content, not just schema. 3) Answer no if only type or schema checks exist, or screening covers some inputs (for example user input) but not tool output, fetched pages, or retrieved chunks."
   },
   {
    "id": "R01.5",
    "check": "Bad payloads are rejected or held aside, and if a check errors, times out, or is skipped, the content is blocked.",
    "verify": "1) Find what happens when validation fails or errors. 2) Read `try`/`except`, `catch`, timeout, and default branches. 3) Answer no if failures log and continue, an error or timeout passes content through, a feature flag can skip checks, or bad content is truncated and still used."
   }
  ]
 },
 "R02": {
  "gate": "G2",
  "title": "Separate data from instructions and test injection containment",
  "lead_role": "security",
  "requirement": "Keep API responses, connector messages, and other outside content out of trusted system and policy channels. Label where each piece came from and how much it is trusted. Test prompt injection in responses that pass schema checks. Include free-text fields, error messages, documents, and tool output. Checks and channel separation can't guarantee that prompt injection is prevented. So show that credential limits, tool permissions, and destructive-action gates still block harm if the model follows an injected instruction. Repeat each attack and include adaptive versions. One failed attempt makes a defense look stronger than it is. B05 covers agents that combine untrusted input, sensitive access, and outside actions.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Missing type, schema, or size checks on API responses",
    "owner": "R01"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Hidden instructions in tool names, descriptions, or schemas",
    "owner": "E04"
   },
   {
    "problem": "One run planting instructions in another run's memory",
    "owner": "R05"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Generated output run as SQL or shell without parameterizing",
    "owner": "R03"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "R02.1",
    "check": "Outside content (API responses, tool output, documents, connector messages) goes into user or tool message roles, never into the system or policy prompt.",
    "verify": "1) Find where model requests are built (`system=`, `messages`, `role: system`, prompt templates). 2) Trace outside content (tool output, documents, API responses) into the request. 3) Answer no if any outside content is concatenated into the system or policy prompt, including retrieved context or tool descriptions from servers."
   },
   {
    "id": "R02.2",
    "check": "Each piece of outside content carries a source and trust label in the model request.",
    "verify": "1) Find where outside content is wrapped before the model request. 2) Check each piece carries a source and trust label. 3) Answer no if some sources (for example tool output or retrieved chunks) have no label, or labels are set by the content itself."
   },
   {
    "id": "R02.3",
    "check": "Credential limits, tool permissions, and B05 action gates block the actions an injected instruction could cause, enforced in code the model cannot change.",
    "verify": "1) List actions an injected instruction could cause: sends, writes, deletes, fetches. 2) Check each is blocked by credential scope, tool allowlist, or B05 gate in harness code. 3) Answer no if any harmful action is stopped only by the model declining, or the limit can be changed by tool arguments or model output."
   },
   {
    "id": "R02.4",
    "check": "Containment does not rely on prompt wording, delimiters, or an injection detector.",
    "verify": "1) Find the defenses relied on for injection. 2) Check whether removing prompt wording, delimiters, or the detector would leave harmful actions blocked. 3) Answer no if containment depends on system-prompt rules, delimiter tags, or a detector, with no code-level block behind it."
   },
   {
    "id": "R02.5",
    "check": "Injection tests repeat each fixture a set number of times, include adaptive variants and schema-valid payloads, and report success rates.",
    "verify": "1) Find injection tests (`tests/`, eval suites, red-team fixtures). 2) Check fixtures repeat a set number of times, include adaptive variants and schema-valid payloads, and report a success rate. 3) Answer no if each test runs once, uses only malformed inputs, or reports pass/fail. Answer unknown if tests live outside this repo."
   }
  ]
 },
 "R03": {
  "gate": "G2",
  "title": "Prevent output and log leakage",
  "lead_role": "agent_team",
  "requirement": "Remove secrets and limit sensitive data before tool output, debug output, or responses reach the model, outside recipients, or normal logs. Some clients fetch URLs on their own when they display output, such as Markdown images and link previews. These can leak data without any tool call, so block them or allow only approved URLs. Check generated output before it goes into SQL, a shell, an HTML page, or anything else that runs it. Use parameterized queries and commands (for example, argument lists for shell calls), and the right escaping for each context. Test with fake secrets and injection payloads. Confirm that leaks and unintended execution are blocked, and that redacted audit evidence is kept.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "A tool call sending data to an unapproved destination",
    "owner": "B09"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Long-lived secrets checked into config or baked into images",
    "owner": "B03"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Incoming hostile payloads not validated",
    "owner": "R01"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Retrieval returning another tenant's documents",
    "owner": "R04"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "R03.1",
    "check": "Secrets and sensitive fields are redacted from tool output, debug output, and error text before they reach the model, outside recipients, or normal logs.",
    "verify": "1) Find redaction code (`redact`, `mask`, secret patterns) and where tool output, errors, and debug text go. 2) Check redaction runs before the model, outside recipients, and logs. 3) Answer no if any path skips it, such as error messages, exception traces, debug logging, or `logger.info(response)` before redaction."
   },
   {
    "id": "R03.2",
    "check": "The display client blocks auto-fetched URLs (Markdown images, link previews) or allows only approved hosts.",
    "verify": "1) Find the display client or renderer (Markdown render, link unfurl, image tags). 2) Check auto-fetched URLs are blocked or limited to approved hosts (CSP `img-src`, URL allowlist). 3) Answer no if Markdown images or previews load any URL. Answer unknown if the display client is outside this repo."
   },
   {
    "id": "R03.3",
    "check": "Generated SQL uses parameterized queries, shell calls use argument lists with no string-built commands, and HTML output is escaped for its context.",
    "verify": "1) Search for SQL, shell, and HTML built from generated output (`execute(f`, `+ query`, `shell=True`, `exec(`, `innerHTML`, `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, `|safe`). 2) Check for parameters, argument lists, and context escaping. 3) Answer no if any query or command is string-built, uses `shell=True` or `sh -c` with model input, or HTML is unescaped."
   },
   {
    "id": "R03.4",
    "check": "Redacted audit evidence is kept rather than discarded.",
    "verify": "1) Find redaction code and audit logging. 2) Check redacted records are written to an audit store. 3) Answer no if redaction drops the whole record, or the evidence is discarded instead of kept."
   }
  ]
 },
 "R04": {
  "gate": "G2",
  "title": "Retrieval-corpus integrity",
  "lead_role": "agent_team",
  "requirement": "The retrieval corpus is the set of documents the agent searches for context. Its index stores those documents, or smaller passages called chunks, in a search engine or vector database. Limit which identities and sources can add or change content there. For each chunk, keep its source, when it was added, its trust level, and its access permissions. Enforce the requesting tenant's access when searching. Test an unapproved attempt to add content and a search across tenants. Also test quarantining, expiring, and revoking access to seeded test documents. Confirm the affected chunks disappear from search results and caches within a stated time. See the [R04 recipe](CHECKLIST-VERIFICATION.md#r--run-time).",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Memory entries poisoned or shared across tenants",
    "owner": "R05"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Agent credentials allowing another tenant's data at the API",
    "owner": "B02"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Retrieved chunks not checked for size, type, or hidden instructions",
    "owner": "R01"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Embedding or reranker model swapped without review",
    "owner": "C05"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "Only when the agent has no retrieval corpus or search index.",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "R04.1",
    "check": "Only named identities and sources can add or change index content, and the ingestion path checks the writer.",
    "verify": "1) Find index write paths (`upsert`, `add_documents`, `index`, ingestion jobs). 2) Check each verifies the writer identity or source against a named list. 3) Answer no if any write path, including the agent's own tools or an admin endpoint, accepts writes without that check."
   },
   {
    "id": "R04.2",
    "check": "Each stored chunk has metadata for source, time added, trust level, and access permissions.",
    "verify": "1) Find chunk write code and metadata fields. 2) Check source, time added, trust level, and access permissions are stored on every chunk. 3) Answer no if any field is missing, or set only on some ingestion paths."
   },
   {
    "id": "R04.3",
    "check": "Search applies the requesting tenant's access filter at query time, in the store or retrieval code, not after the model answers.",
    "verify": "1) Find search calls (`similarity_search`, `query`, `search`, `filter=`, `where=`). 2) Check a tenant or access filter is applied in the store query, from the caller's identity. 3) Answer no if filtering happens after retrieval or in the prompt, the filter is optional, or the tenant comes from a model-supplied argument."
   },
   {
    "id": "R04.4",
    "check": "Code can quarantine, expire, and revoke chunks, and it also clears or invalidates search caches.",
    "verify": "1) Find quarantine, expiry, and revoke code (`delete`, `quarantine`, `ttl`, `expires_at`). 2) Check search excludes those chunks and caches are cleared. 3) Answer no if any of the three operations is missing, or caches (query cache, embedding cache) are not invalidated."
   }
  ]
 },
 "R05": {
  "gate": "G3",
  "title": "Memory isolation and write validation",
  "lead_role": "agent_team",
  "requirement": "Separate memory by tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed. Block access across those lines unless it is clearly approved. Check what gets written to memory. Test that one poisoned run can't quietly plant instructions in another run's memory. Don't move the agent's own output into trusted memory without review, and let unverified entries expire. Write checks must also cover content that arrives through ordinary queries, not just special write paths.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "No record of who wrote a memory entry, or no way to remove it",
    "owner": "R06"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Retrieval search not filtered by the requesting tenant",
    "owner": "R04"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Agent credentials allowing another tenant's data at the API",
    "owner": "B02"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Rollback restoring old memory or index state unchecked",
    "owner": "C07"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "Only when the agent has no memory that lasts between runs.",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "R05.1",
    "check": "Memory reads and writes are scoped by tenant, agent type, instance, and task as needed, and cross-scope access is denied unless explicitly allowed.",
    "verify": "1) Find memory read and write code (`memory`, `store`, `namespace`, keys). 2) Check keys include tenant, agent type, instance, or task as needed. 3) Answer no if keys omit tenant, the scope comes from a model argument, or reads fall back to a shared or global namespace."
   },
   {
    "id": "R05.2",
    "check": "Every memory write, including content arriving through ordinary queries, passes validation before it is stored.",
    "verify": "1) List every memory write path, including automatic saves from ordinary conversation. 2) Check each passes validation before storing. 3) Answer no if any path writes without validation, or validation errors are caught and the write continues."
   },
   {
    "id": "R05.3",
    "check": "The agent's own output cannot reach trusted or shared memory without a review step.",
    "verify": "1) Find where agent output is written to shared or trusted memory. 2) Check a review step gates the write. 3) Answer no if agent output goes straight into shared or trusted memory, or the review flag defaults to approved."
   },
   {
    "id": "R05.4",
    "check": "Unverified entries have an expiry that is enforced.",
    "verify": "1) Find how unverified entries are marked and expired (`ttl`, `expires_at`, cleanup jobs). 2) Check reads filter out expired entries. 3) Answer no if no expiry is set, it is set but never enforced on read or by a job, or the default is no expiry."
   }
  ]
 },
 "R06": {
  "gate": "G3",
  "title": "Memory provenance and cleanup",
  "lead_role": "agent_team",
  "requirement": "For any memory entry, look up who wrote it, when, for which task, from what input, and the context size at the time. Show that you can find, quarantine, and remove bad entries, and control their reuse in later sessions.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Memory not separated by tenant, agent type, instance, or task",
    "owner": "R05"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Poisoned retrieval documents not quarantined or expired",
    "owner": "R04"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Context size not logged for each action decision",
    "owner": "R07"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded",
    "owner": "A06"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "Only when the agent has no memory that lasts between runs.",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "R06.1",
    "check": "Each memory entry stores its writer, time, task, source input, and context size at write time.",
    "verify": "1) Find the memory entry schema. 2) Check fields for writer, time, task, source input, and context size. 3) Answer no if any is missing, or set only on some write paths."
   },
   {
    "id": "R06.2",
    "check": "Derived entries such as summaries keep links to the entries they came from.",
    "verify": "1) Find summary or derived-entry creation code. 2) Check it stores links to source entry IDs. 3) Answer no if derived entries keep no source links."
   },
   {
    "id": "R06.3",
    "check": "Code can find entries by source or writer and quarantine or delete them, along with derived entries and caches.",
    "verify": "1) Find code that queries by source or writer and quarantines or deletes. 2) Check it also handles derived entries and caches. 3) Answer no if lookup by source or writer is impossible, or derived entries and caches are left in place."
   },
   {
    "id": "R06.4",
    "check": "Quarantined or deleted entries are excluded from retrieval in later sessions.",
    "verify": "1) Find memory retrieval code. 2) Check it excludes quarantined or deleted entries in new sessions. 3) Answer no if retrieval ignores the quarantine flag, or a cache or index still returns removed entries."
   }
  ]
 },
 "R07": {
  "gate": "G2",
  "title": "Decision-time context size",
  "lead_role": "agent_team",
  "requirement": "For each action, log how full the model's context was when it decided, in tokens. Document how you count. Say whether each value was measured, estimated, or unavailable. Cover cached input, compaction (shrinking the context), and limits the provider hides. Per-call billing totals don't automatically equal context size. Use them only if your counting method shows they match. A missing value is a clear gap, never zero. Check that the value survives tool handoffs and can be searched next to the action's identity and result. See the [telemetry conventions](otel-conventions.md).",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Behavior not compared across context-size ranges",
    "owner": "R09"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Token or cost limits not enforced outside the model",
    "owner": "B07"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Action records missing any of the six identity fields",
    "owner": "A02"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "R07.1",
    "check": "Each action record includes the model's context size in tokens at decision time.",
    "verify": "1) Find action or tool-call records (trace spans, audit logs). 2) Check each has context size in tokens at decision time. 3) Answer no if the field is missing or recorded only per run, not per action."
   },
   {
    "id": "R07.2",
    "check": "Each value is labeled measured, estimated, or unavailable, and a missing value is stored as unknown, never as zero.",
    "verify": "1) Find where context size is recorded. 2) Check a label of measured, estimated, or unavailable, and how missing values are stored. 3) Answer no if there is no label, or a missing value defaults to `0` (`or 0`, `?? 0`, `.get(..., 0)`)."
   },
   {
    "id": "R07.3",
    "check": "The count comes from a stated method that handles cached input and compaction, not a copied per-call billing total.",
    "verify": "1) Find how context size is counted. 2) Check a stated method covers cached input and compaction. 3) Answer no if it copies `usage.input_tokens` or a billing total with no stated method that matches it, or ignores cache and compaction."
   },
   {
    "id": "R07.4",
    "check": "The value is carried across tool handoffs and stored with the action's identity, result, and trace ID.",
    "verify": "1) Trace context size through tool handoffs and sub-agent calls. 2) Check it is stored with action identity, result, and trace ID. 3) Answer no if it is lost on handoff, or stored separately with no link to the action."
   }
  ]
 },
 "R08": {
  "gate": "G3",
  "title": "Security monitoring",
  "lead_role": "security",
  "requirement": "Keep security rules and baselines separate from answer-quality monitoring. Test encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, writes outside scope, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls. Check that alerts reach a named responder.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Chains of individually allowed actions going undetected",
    "owner": "R09"
   },
   {
    "problem": "No test of a second-model review against evasive behavior",
    "owner": "R10"
   },
   {
    "problem": "No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type",
    "owner": "E12"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Rate and cost limits not enforced outside the model",
    "owner": "B07"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "R08.1",
    "check": "Security detection rules and baselines are defined separately from answer-quality monitoring.",
    "verify": "1) Find detection rules and monitoring config (SIEM rules, alert config, eval dashboards). 2) Check security rules are separate from answer-quality metrics. 3) Answer no if only quality monitoring exists. Answer unknown if monitoring lives outside this repo."
   },
   {
    "id": "R08.2",
    "check": "Rules exist for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls.",
    "verify": "1) Read the security detection rules. 2) Check for encoded payloads, unexpected outbound traffic, out-of-scope writes, privilege escalation, and repeated denied calls. 3) Answer no if any is missing, or denied calls are never logged so the rule cannot fire."
   },
   {
    "id": "R08.3",
    "check": "Alerts from these rules route to a named responder or on-call target in config.",
    "verify": "1) Find alert routing (PagerDuty, Opsgenie, alertmanager receivers, on-call config). 2) Check the security rules route to a named responder. 3) Answer no if alerts go only to a dashboard or log, or the receiver is empty. Answer unknown if routing lives outside this repo."
   }
  ]
 },
 "R09": {
  "gate": "G3",
  "title": "Sequence and context-aware detection",
  "lead_role": "security",
  "requirement": "Test suspicious chains of actions that are each allowed on their own. Also test unusual fan-out and resource use. Compare behavior across context-size ranges, so one fleet-wide baseline doesn't hide failures in long contexts. If the agent could be hijacked while using only allowed tools, check each high-impact action against the original task before it runs. Use a checker that doesn't see the untrusted content in between, and measure what it misses.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Encoded payloads or writes outside scope going undetected",
    "owner": "R08"
   },
   {
    "problem": "No test against a deliberately evasive, misaligned agent",
    "owner": "R10"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Context size not logged for each action decision",
    "owner": "R07"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "R09.1",
    "check": "Detection rules evaluate sequences of calls across a run (for example many reads followed by an outside send), not only single calls.",
    "verify": "1) Read detection rules. 2) Check rules evaluate sequences across a run, such as many reads then an outside send. 3) Answer no if every rule looks at a single call or event."
   },
   {
    "id": "R09.2",
    "check": "Rules cover unusual fan-out and resource use.",
    "verify": "1) Read detection rules. 2) Check rules for unusual fan-out (sub-agents, parallel calls) and resource use. 3) Answer no if neither is covered."
   },
   {
    "id": "R09.3",
    "check": "Thresholds or baselines are set per context-size range, not as one fleet-wide value.",
    "verify": "1) Read rule thresholds and baselines. 2) Check they are set per context-size range. 3) Answer no if one fleet-wide value is used for all contexts."
   },
   {
    "id": "R09.4",
    "check": "If hijack through allowed tools alone is possible, each high-impact action is checked against the original task before it runs, by a checker that is not given the untrusted content.",
    "verify": "1) Check whether allowed tools alone could cause harm if the agent is hijacked. 2) If so, find a task-alignment checker before high-impact actions and see what input it receives. 3) Answer no if no checker exists, it is given the untrusted content, or its errors let the action proceed."
   }
  ]
 },
 "R10": {
  "gate": "G3",
  "title": "Misaligned-behavior review",
  "lead_role": "security",
  "requirement": "If deliberate misalignment is in scope, test a second model or pattern review against realistic evasive behavior. Record what it can and can't catch. Include what the reviewer can see: actions, arguments, and any visible reasoning. Reasoning the reviewer can't see is a stated gap. Include red-team runs by an agent that knows it is being watched. This review must not replace rule-based access and action controls that don't depend on a model's judgment. Deeper checks on intent belong in a separate AI-control program at the model level.",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Destructive actions not blocked by a rule-based gate",
    "owner": "B05"
   },
   {
    "problem": "A swapped, drifting, or failing checker model not treated as a failure",
    "owner": "A07"
   },
   {
    "problem": "Chains of individually allowed actions going undetected",
    "owner": "R09"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "Only when deliberate misalignment is recorded as out of scope for this deployment, with a reason and approval.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "R11": {
  "gate": "G1",
  "title": "Tested kill switch",
  "lead_role": "operations",
  "requirement": "Name who may stop the agent and how. The stop path must not depend on the agent's cooperation. The agent's credentials must not be able to change it. Set a maximum stop time that fits the potential harm. Run a dated drill on the current deployment. It must stop new actions, running work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks. Include an attempt by the agent to disable or delay the stop. Cover sub-agents and their children in the ecosystem drill (E09).",
  "not_covered": [
   {
    "problem": "Stop signal not reaching sub-agents, children, or remote workers",
    "owner": "E09"
   },
   {
    "problem": "In-flight writes, queued actions, or unattended restarts after the stop",
    "owner": "R12"
   },
   {
    "problem": "No way to cut off the agent's access without cutting off the user",
    "owner": "B04"
   }
  ],
  "na_when": "never",
  "checks": [
   {
    "id": "R11.1",
    "check": "A stop control halts new model calls and new tool calls when set.",
    "verify": "1) Find the stop control (kill flag, `stop`, `halt`, feature flag, revoked token). 2) Check the loop checks it before every model call and tool call. 3) Answer no if it is checked only at run start, only between turns, a second loop or worker skips it, or a check error is treated as not stopped."
   },
   {
    "id": "R11.2",
    "check": "The agent's own credentials and tools cannot set, clear, delete, or delay the stop control.",
    "verify": "1) Find where the stop flag is stored and who can write it. 2) Compare with the agent's credentials and tools. 3) Answer no if the agent's role can write, delete, or expire the flag, a tool can reach its store, or the agent can delay it (for example by catching the stop signal)."
   },
   {
    "id": "R11.3",
    "check": "Tool calls already running are cancelled or bounded by a timeout when the stop is set.",
    "verify": "1) Find the stop handler. 2) Check running tool calls are cancelled or bounded by a timeout. 3) Answer no if running calls finish with no timeout, or cancellation errors are swallowed and the call continues."
   },
   {
